# tda.hlp
# This file contains the entries for TDA's help command. The
# conventions are: Lines beginning with two consecutive # characters
# mark the begin of a new key word. The corresponding entry runs
# until the next key word, or until end of file. Lines beginning
# with a single # character are comments and skipped when reading
# the file. With the exception of "version", the ordering of key
# words is alphabetical. 

##version
This help file is for TDA 6.4e
   
##$
$: The command

    $ string;

calls a shell in order to execute string. If string contains blank
characters it must be put into single or double quotation marks.
##arcc
arcc: The command

    arcc;

checks whether TDA can successfully read a data archive that has been
opened with the arcd command. The command also checks whether all
variable names in the variable description file are unique.

See also: arcd
##arcd
arcd: The command

    arcd = name_of_an_archive_description_file;

opens access to a data archive described by the specified archive
description file. Access to the archive remains until explicitly 
turned off with

    arcd = off;

or until another arcd command is used to open a new archive. If used without
arguments, the arcd command provides information about the currently defined
data archive.

See also: arcc arcv arcvc
##arcv
arcv: The command

    arcv(
       fn = ...,            optional name of a data file
       arcdic,              request value label information
    ) = fname;              output file name

writes information about variables, available in a currently open data
archive, into fname. By default, information is given for all variables.
If a file name is specified with the fn parameter, information is only
for variables contained in that file.

See also: arcd
##arcvc
arcvc: The command

    arcvc(
        df  = ...,      name of new variable description file
        opt = ...,      option for non-unique variable names, def. 1
                        1 = append logical file number
                        2 = append file name in lower case letters
                        3 = append file name in upper case letters
                
    ) = name_of_a_variable_description_file;

can be used to check the integrity of a variable description file and
create a new variable description file with unique variable names. For
further information see Section 2.10.4 of the Manual.
##atab
atab: The atab can be used for aggregating the values of a variable and,
in particular, for creating histograms. The syntax is:
  
    atab(      
        x=...,       definition of classes, no default    
        fmt=...,     print format, def. 10.4    
        s=1,         classes left open, def. s=0 (right open)    
        r=1,         print only nonempty classes 
        mppar=...,   write parameters into matrix
    ) = varlist;               

varlist, on the right-hand side, must specify one or two variables.

See also: mppar, ploth
##balanced repeated replication
balanced repeated replication: see brr
##block data structure
block data structure: see dblock.
##becl
becl: Bond energy clustering.

    becl(     
        alg=...,   algorithm, def. 1
                   1 = only column permutations
                   2 = only row permutations
                   3 = row and column permutations
        min=...,   1 if minimization of objective function
        cn=...,    list of starting nodes, def. 1 
        gn=...,    graph number, def. 1 
        opt=...,   output option, def. 1
                   1 = permuted node list 
                   2 = edge list          
                   3 = permuted D, lower triangle
                   4 = permuted D, square matrix 
        sc=...,    substitute for missing values, def. -1
                   (only with opt = 3, 4)            
        nfmt=...,  integer print format, def. 4 
        fmt=...,   print format for values, def. 10.4
    ) = fname;          

The command requires an undirected valued graph. All parameters, except for
the name of an output file on the right-hand side, are optional.
##break
break: The break command can be used inside of repeat/endrepeat and
while/endwhile loops. All commands until the next matching endrepeat
or endwhile command are then skipped.

See also: if, repeat, while
##brr
brr: The brr command can be used for balanced repeated replications.
The syntax is:

     brr(
       ptab = fname,
     ) = ns,nu;

where ns is number of strata and nu is number of secu. This command shows
the number of required replications on standard output. If the optional
ptab parameter is used, the BRR indicators are written into the output file
fname. These data will be identical to those that can be directly created
with the matrix command

    mbrr(ns,nu,S);

This command creates a ns x nr matrix, S, where ns is the number of strata
and nr the number of replications, and

    S(i,j) = SECU in strata i for replication j.

For example, assume a data matrix with variables Strata and SECU. Then, 
in order to get BRR replications, one can use the following commands:

    dblock = Strata;            creates ns blocks
    mbrr(ns,nu,S);              creates the S matrix
    repsel(v=SECU) = S(j);      jth replication

##case selection
case selection: See: tsel   

##case weights
case weights: see: cwt

##cblen
cblen: The command     

    cblen=...

can be used to change the size of the command buffer, default is 20000.
The command can only be used in the command line when invoking the program.

See also: limitations
##ccnt
ccnt: The command

    ccnt = fname;

writes the frequency distribution of the characters contained in the file
fname into standard output.

See also: lcnt, dump
##clear
clear: The command

    clear;

without arguments deletes the current data matrix. In order to delete
selected variables one can use the command

    clear = varlist;

where varlist is a comma-separated list of variable names.
##clearnl
clearnl: The command

    clearnl = list_of_namelists;

deletes the namelists specified on the right-hand side.
##clustering procedures      
clustering procedures: The following commands are available.

Command                                                     Section
-------------------------------------------------------------------
  becl      bond energy clustering                          7.5.3.1
  hcld      simple divisive clustering                      7.5.2.1
  hcld      partition with minimal diameter                 7.5.2.2
  hcls      SAHN clustering procedures                      7.5.1.1
  hclsp     construction of partitions                      7.5.1.1
  nncl      nearest-neighbor clustering                     7.5.1.2

##cmean
cmean: The type 2 operator cmean(A,F) calculates the conditional mean
of A given weights F. If a new variable, say M = cmean(A,F), is defined,         

                   sum_j=i^n A(j) * F(j)
            M(i) = ---------------------
                       sum_j=i^n F(j)

##com
com: The command              

    com(
        opt=...,    option, def. 1
                    1 = all orderes n-tupels
                    2 = all m-sets that can be created from n integers
                    3 = all m-tuples 
                    4 = all permutations of n numbers
                    5 = all partitions of n
                    6 = all partitions of n into m parts (m greater than 1)
                    7 = all partitions of n into m subsets
        n=...,      def. 1
        m=...,      def. 1
    ) = fname;

    creates combinatorial patterns. The patterns are written into the
    output file defined by fname.

##combinatorial optimization
combinatorial optimization: The following commands are available.

Command                                                     Section
-------------------------------------------------------------------
  gap       column permutations                             7.3.1.1
  gqap      quadratic assignment                            7.3.1.2

