##contingency measures
contingency measures: The freq2 command can be used to calculate various
contingency indicators for a two-dimensional table.
##corr
corr: The corr command can be used to calculate the correlation matrix for
a set of variables. The syntax is:

    corr(      
        fmt=...,      print format, def. 7.4    
        df=...,       output file          
        prn=...,      data format in output file, def. 0
        mpcov=...,    write result into matrix
    ) = varlist;

If varlist is not specified, the command uses all currently defined
variables.

There are five possible output formats:

    prn = 0   lower triangle and main diagonal          
          1   full square matrix          
          2   column vector: lower triangle          
          3   column vector: lower triangle and main diagonal          
          4   column vector: full matrix          

See also: cov, mpcov, rcorr

##correspondence analysis
correspondence analysis. See: dma

##cov
cov: The cov command can be used to calculate the covariance matrix for
a set of variables. The syntax is:

    cov(    
        fmt=...,      print format, def. 7.4    
        df=...,       output file          
        prn=...,      data format in output file, def. 0           
        mpcov=...,    write result into matrix
    ) = varlist;             

If varlist is not specified, the command uses all currently defined
variables.

There are five possible output formats:

    prn = 0   lower triangle and main diagonal          
          1   full square matrix          
          2   column vector: lower triangle          
          3   column vector: lower triangle and main diagonal          
          4   column vector: full matrix          

See also: corr, mpcov
##cro
cro: The command

    cro(
       m = ...,   size of rank orders, def. 2
    ) = fname;

creates all rank orders of size m (greater than 1), including all possible
tie groups. The rank orders are written into the output file defined by
fname. The file contains m + 2 columns. First column is record number,
second column is number of tie groups, then follows the rank order.

##cwt
cwt: The cwt command can be used to define case weights. Syntax is:

    cwt = W;   

where W is the name of a variable which must be contained in the current
data matrix. The values of W are then interpreted as weights for the
corresponding rows of the data matrix. These values must be non-negative,
and the sum of weights for all cases in the data matrix, or cases selected
with the tsel command, must be strictly positive.

Alternatively, one can use the command as

    cwt (wnorm=s) = W;  

where s is a positive number. The weights are then modified in such a way
that the sum of weights for the currently selected data matrix rows becomes
equal to s. When simply using

    cwt (wnorm) = W;   

the sum of weights becomes equal to the currently selected number of cases.
A specification of case weights remains active until substituted by 
another cwt command, or explicitly turned off with the command

    cwt = off;       
##data
data: The command

    data;

without arguments provides information about the current data matrix.

See also: data1
##data1
data1: The command

    data1;

without arguments provides information about the currently available 
variables and namelists.

See also: data
##data archive
data archive: See: arcd arcc arcv

##dblock
dblock: The command

    dblock ( mdef=... ) = varlist;

creates a block data structure. All parameters are optional. The command
first turns off any possibly active tsel (temporary case) selection. It
then creates a data structure that keeps record of data matrix blocks defined
by the variables in varlist. Blocks are defined as contiguous blocks of data
matrix rows where the variables in varlist have identical values; or, if
varlist is empty, each data matrix row is treated as a separate block.

The dblock command sets the global variable BNOC to the number of blocks
that has been found. This number is available with the type 1 operator

    bnoc

If the optional parameter 

    mdef = M,                  

is used, where M is the name of a matrix, the dblock command creates a
NOC x 1 matrix named M and sets
                
    M(i,1) = block number of case i.

See also: repsel
##direct sum
direct sum: see mcathv.

##distance matrix
distance matrix: see pdatd

##dma
dma: The dma command can be used for variants of principal components analysis. 
The syntax is:

    dma(
        v=...,         varlist, def.: all variables
        alg=...,       algorithm, def. 1
                        1  principal components (based on data matrix)
                        2  principal components (based on cov./correlation matrix)
                        3  factor analysis (based on data matrix)
                        4  factor analysis (based on correlation matrix)
                        5  dual scaling (based on frequency table)
                        6  correspondence analysis (based on frequency table)
                        7  direct svd-based projection
        opt=...,       preprocessing (only for algorithms 1 and 7), def. 1
                         for alg = 1:
                           1  do nothing
                           2  mean centering of variables
                           3  standardization of variables
                         for alg = 5 and 7:
                           1  do nothing
                           2  relative frequencies of rows
                           3  relative frequencies of table
        prn=1;         additional standard output, def. 0
        df=...,        write eigenvectors into specified output file
        fmt=...,       print format, def. 10.4
        ns=...,        write max. ... components/eigenvectors, def. all
        pcf=...,       plot command file (only algorithm 6)
    ) [= output file]; optional output file

The command creates its input data from the current TDA data matrix. Variables can
be selected with the v parameter. By default, the command uses all variables.

(1) Algorithm 1 assumes that the input data, say X, is a standard data matrix with n
    n rows (cases) and m columns (variables). Optionally the columns of X are mean-
    centered (opt=2) or standardized (opt=3). The command creates A = (X'X)/(n-1) 
    and performs a spectral decomposition AV = VL, where V is the (m,m) matrix of
    eigenvectors and L is a (m,m) diagonal matrix containing the eigenvalues. The
    eigenvalues are shown in the standard output. Optionally, the eigenvectors are
    written into an output file that can be requested with the df parameter, and the
    principal components, XV, are written into the output file specified on the
    right-hand side of the command.

    If prn=1, the input data after preprocessing, the cross-product matrix,
    and the eigenvectors are written into the standard output.

(2) Algorithm 2 assumes that the input data, say A, is a symmetric semi-positive
    definite matrix (a covariance or correlation matrix). It then performs a
    spectral decomposition in the same way as described for algorithm 1. Output
    options are also the same except for principal components which cannot be
    calculated.

    If prn=1, the cross-product matrix and the eigenvectors are written
    into the standard output.

(3) Algorithm 3 assumes that the input data, say X, is a standard data matrix with 
    n rows (cases) and m columns (variables). The command standardizes the columns
    of X, creates the correlation matrix A = (X'X)/(n-1), and performs a spectral
    decomposition AV = VL. The eigenvectots are re-scaled such that V'V = L in order
    to allow an interpretation as factor loadings. The eigenvalues are shown in the
    standard output. The rescaled eigenvectors are written into an output file if
    requested with the df parameter. The factors, Y = L^(-1)XV, are written into
    an output file specified on the right hand side of the command.
  
    If prn=1, the correlation matrix and the factor loading (rescaled
    eigenvectors) are written into the standard output.

(4) Algorithm 4 assumes that the input data, say A, is a correlation matrix. The
    calculations are the same as described for algorithm 3. Also the output
    options are the same except for factors which cannot be calculated.

    If prn=1, the correlation matrix and the factor loading (rescaled
    eigenvectors) are written into the standard output.

(5) Algorithm 5 assumes that the input data, say X, is an (n,m) frequency
    table. If opt = 2, each row of X is transformed into a distribution
    (sum of row entries = 1), if opt = 3, X is transformed into a
    distribution (sum of table entries = 1). The algorithm then calculates
    a new matrix A with elements

        a(i,j) = x(i,j) / sqrt( x(i,+) x(+,j) )

    Then follows a SVD (singular value decomposition) of A, say A = UQV', where
    Q is a (n,n) diagonal matrix containing the singular values of A. V contains
    the eigenvectors of A'A, and U contains the eigenvectors of AA'. Optimal rows
    scores are calculated as columns of a matrix R with elements

        r(i,j) = u(i,j) / sqrt( x(i,+) )

    Optimal column scores are calculated as columns of a matrix C with elements

        c(i,j) = v(i,j) / sqrt( x(+,i) )

    The A-matrix is written into the file specified with the df parameter.
    If prn=1, the frequency table is written into the standard output.

(6) Algorithm 6 assumes that the input data, say X, is an (n,m) frequency table.
    The algorithm transforms X to relative frequencies (sum of table elements = 1)
    and then calculates a new matrix A with elements 

        a(i,j) = (x(i,j) - x(i,+) x(+,j)) / sqrt( x(i,+) x(+,j) )

    i.e, standardized residuals. The next step is a singular value decomposition
    A = UQV'. Finally, the algorithm calculates principal and standard
    coordinates as described by M. Greenacre and J. Blasius, eds., Correspondence
    Analysis in the Social Sciences (Academic Press 1994), pp. 53-78. Singular
    values, eigenvalues ("principal inertias"), and coordinates are displayed in
    the standard output. If requested, row coordinates are written in an output
    file specified with the df parameter and column coordinates are written in
    an output file specied on the right hand side. Furthermore, one can use
    the pcf parameter to request a TDA command file that creates a plot of the
    correspondence analysis.

    If prn=1, the frequency table and the standardized residuals are
    written into the standard output.

(7) Algorithm 7 assumes a standard (n,m) data matrix X (n >= m). If 
    opt = 2, each row of X is transformed into a distribution (sum of
    row entries = 1), if opt = 3, X is transformed into a distribution
    (sum of table entries = 1). The algorithm then performs a singular       
    value decomposition X = UQV' and calculates coordinates XV from the
    first two columns of V (corresponding to the two largest singular
    values). Coordinates are written into the standard output and optionally
    to the output file specified on the right-hand side of the command.

    If prn=1, the input data are written into the standard output.

##dmet
dmet: Change distance matrix intro metric. The command requires an           
undirected valued graph. The syntax is:                              

    dmet(                                                  
      gn = ...,   graph number, def. 1                     
      fmt=...,    printf format, def. 10.4  
   ) = fname;                                              

The command calculates c = max{0, max_i,j,k {d_i,j - d_i,k - d_kj}}                     
and changes the distances d_ij into d_ij + c. Both the original and
the modified distance matrices are written into the output file.

##dple
dple: Product-limit estimation with discrete times.
                                                                   
    dple(                                                      
      df=...,         output file (required)                 
      fmt=...,        print format, def. 12.4                
      opt=...,        output option, def. 1                  
                      1 : all time points                     
                      2 : only time points occurring in T    
      grp=...,        list of group variables                
    ) = [S,] T, D;                                             
                                                                 
There must be two or three variable names on the right-hand side       
defining durations on a process time axis beginning at zero. S is the  
beginning of the observation period (zero if not specified), and T is  
the duration, if D is not equal to zero, and otherwise is the end of   
the observation period. It is assumed that values of S and T are       
integers, furthermore: 0 <= S <= T.                                    
                                                                 
For each time point t (min(S) <= t <= max(T)), the command counts:       
  e(t) = number of events at t, that is, the number of cases i with  
         T(i) = t and D(i) != 0; and                                 
  r(t) = number of elements in the risk set at t, that is, the number
         of cases i with S(i) <= t and T(i) >= t                     
                                                                   
Finally, for each time point t, one record is written to the output    
file specified by the df parameter, containing the following columns:  
  (1)  index of table                                                
  (2)  time                                                            
  (3)  r(t)                                                          
  (4)  e(t)                                                          
  (5)  number of censored cases                                      
  (6)  the rate function e(t) / r(t)                                 
  (7)  the survivor function calculated as the product of            
       (1 - e(j)/r(j)) for j < t.                                    
                                                                 
If the grp parameter is used to specify groups, the calculations are     
done separately for each group. If opt = 1, the output tables contain  
one row for each time point beginning at min(S) and ending at max(T).  
If opt = 2, only rows with at least one observation (value of T) are     
printed.                                                               

Note: The dple command recognizes case weights defined with the cwt
command. Weights might be integer or floating point values. 

##dplot
dplot: The dplot command can be used to combine several TDA PostScript
files into a single PostScript file. The syntax is:

    dplot(
        fn=...,      list of input files    
        pxlen=...,   x-size of output plot in mm, def. 120    
        pylen=...,   y-size of output plot in mm, def.  80    
        psorg=...,   origin of output plot, def. psorg=10,10
    ) = fname;
      
The right-hand side must provide the name of an output file, and the
fn parameter must be used to specify at least one set of PostScript
files previously created with TDA. These files are placed in one
row of plots in the output file. If used more than once (up to 50),
each set of files is placed into a separate row of plots.        
##dsplit
dsplit: The dsplit command can be used to split a file into parts.          
Syntax is:

    dsplit (len=...) = fname; 
                  
where fname is the name of a file. The size of the parts can be defined
with the parameter len, default is len=1000. The file specified by fname
is split into parts of n bytes. The parts are named:

    fname.a, fname.b, fname.c, and so on. 

##dstat
dstat: The dstat command calculates elementary descriptive statistics.
The syntax is:

    dstat(      
        grp=...,     group variables   
        fmt=...,     print format, def. 10.4    
        df=...,      output file           
        mppar=...,   write parameters into matrix
    ) = varlist;     

varlist is optional. If not specified, the command uses all currently
defined variables.

See also: grp, mppar
##dummy variable 
dummy variable: Individual dummy variables can be defined by using the [.]
operator. For example, Y = X[3] would define a dummy variable Y, taking
the value 1 if X has value 3, and zero otherwise. 

If one has to create a long series of dummy variables and possibly also
interaction effects, it is sometimes more comfortable to use the ndvar
command.

See also: ndvar
##dump
dump: The command

    dump (nc=..., s=...) = fname;

writes the binary contents of the file fname into standard output.
By default, the command  begins with the first character in the input      
file. Alternatively, it begins with an offset given by the s parameter.
The nc parameter can be used to limit the number of characters; by
default, all characters of the input file are used.
##edef
edef: The edef command can be used to define an episode data structure.
The syntax is:

    edef(     
        org=...,        origin state     
        des=...,        destination state     
        ts=...,         starting time    
        tf=...,         ending time      
        id=...,         ID number        
        sn=...,         spell number     
        maxtran=...,    maximum number of transitions, def. 100    
        VName=...,      definition of type 5 variables   
        ...             can be repeated                  
        split=...,      variables for episode splitting    
    );      

If used without arguments, the edef command provides information about currently 
defined episode data.

##ejoin  
ejoin: The command

    ejoin(
        if1 = ...,          first input file name
        if2 = ...,          second input file name
        max = ...,          max block size, def. 1000 records
        nw  = ...,          max number of levels, def. 1
        len = ...,          max record length, def. 1000
        noc = ...,          read max noc records, def. all
        fmt0=...,           print format for first 8 entries
        fmt1=...,           print format for covariates file 1
        fmt1=...,           print format for covariates file 2
    ) = name of output file;

    can be used to process and, optionally, join event data files.
    There must be at least one input file. Input files must be free
    format files where the first 6 entries are as follows (all entries
    are assumed to be integers with a maximum of 9 digits):

    Col 1 -  case Id used to identify blocks 
    Col 2 -  number of records (spells) in current block
    Col 3 -  record (spell) number in current block
    Col 4 -  starting time of spell
    Col 5 -  ending time of spell
    Col 6 -  state (non-negative)

    Any further entries in the file are treated as covariables.
    Note: the input files should be sorted first w.r.t. case Id,
    and secondly, inside each block, w.r.t. the starting time.

    If spells are overlapping, the command performs episode
    splitting and creates as many levels as necessary. (This
    required an approprite setting of the nw parameter.)

    The output file has the following entries:

    Col 1 -  case Id used to identify blocks
    Col 2 -  number of records (spells) in current block
    Col 3 -  record (spell) number in current block
    Col 4 -  level number (0,1,2,...)
    Col 5 -  starting time of spell
    Col 6 -  ending time of spell
    Col 7 -  state in file 1      
    Col 8 -  state in file 2  (if there is a second input file)
    Additional covariables from first file
    Additional covariables from second file
##emerge
emerge: The emerge command can be used to merge big data files that do not
fit into main memory. The syntax is:

    emerge(
        df=...,      name of output file (required) 
        mf=...,      list of merge files  
        len=...,     maximal record length, def. 1000 
        m=...,       missing value character, def. blank 
        sepc=...,    separation character, def. blank 
    ) = input_file;

The command expects the name of a standard ASCII data file on its
right-hand side. This file must have valid end-of-record characters.
The maximal record length can be specified with the len parameter.
Also required is the name of an output file to be given with the df
parameter. Finally, at least one more file to be merged with the
input file must be specified with the mf parameter. Syntax is:

    mf = fname1[i1,j1,i,j],   
       = fname2[i2,j2,i,j],   
       = fname3[i3,j3,i,j], ...,         

Up to 50 file names can be specified on the right-hand side. (Note, however,
that this maximum may be less due to limitations of the operating system.)
Like the main input file, these files must be plain ASCII files with
valid end-of-record characters and record length not exceeding len. In
addition, for each of these files one has to specify two fields. The first
two numbers, ik and jk, must specify, respectively, the first and last
columns of a field in the records of the corresponding file to be merged
(i.e., fname_k). The second two numbers, i and j, must specify a field in
the main input file. While the (ik,jk) fields can be different for each file,
there can only be a single field in the main input file. Furthermore, all
fields must have the same length.

The command expects that all input files are already sorted with respect
to the specified fields. 

See also: esort, eselect, eskip
##epdat 
epdat: The epdat command can be used to write episode data into an
output file. The syntax is:

    epdat(      
        noc=...,     maximum number of cases     
        fmt=...,     print format for ts and tf, def. 6.2      
        v=...,       list of additional variables      
        ap=1,        append new data to end of output file   
        dtda=...,    TDA description of output file 
    ) = fname;      

##episode data
episode data: See: edef epdat 

##epsdat
epsdat: The epsdat can be used to calculate state distributions for
a set of episode data. The syntax is:

    epsdat(     
       t=...,      sequence of time points. 
       ap=1,       append new data to end of output file
    ) = fname;     

The command requires a definition of episode data with the edef command.

See also: time periods
##eselect
eselect: The eselect command can be used to select records from a big data
file based on keys provided by another file. The syntax is:

    eselect(
        df=...,     name of output file (required)   
        if=...,     additional input file     
        sk=...,     specification of keys 
        noc=...,    max number of keys, def. 1000
        opt=...,    if 1 (def.) write all records
                    if 2 supress identical records
        len=...,    maximal record length, def. 1000
    ) = input_file;
                
The command expects the name of a standard ASCII data file on its
right-hand side. This file must have valid end-of-record characters.
The maximal record length can be specified with the len parameter.
Also required is the name of an output file to be given with the df
parameter.
                            
In addition, the command expects another input file to be specified with
the if parameter. Again, this must be a standard ASCII file with valid
end-of-record characters and record length not exceeding len. The
parameter

    sk = i1,i2,j1,j2,

must be used to specify two fields. i1 and i2 specify, respectively, a
field beginning in column i1 and ending in column i2 in the records
of the main input file. j1 and j2 specify a corresponding field in the
records of the additional input file defined with the if parameter. Both
fields must have the same length. The maximal number of fields (keys) read
from the additional input file can be specified with the noc paramater.

See also: esort, emerge, eskip
##eskip  
eskip: The eskip command can be used to drop selected columns from
a big file. The syntax is:

    eskip(
        df=...,     name of output file (required)
        sk=...,     up to five fields    
        len=...,    maximal record length, def. 1000 
    ) = input_file;
     
The command expects the name of a standard ASCII data file on its
right-hand side. This file must have valid end-of-record characters.
The maximal record length can be specified with the len parameter.
Also required is the name of an output file to be given with the df
parameter. In addition, the command expects a specification of up to
five fields with the parameter

    sk = i1,j1,i2,j2,...,    

The kth field begins in column ik and ends in column jk. The command
sequentially reads the records from the input file and writes those
columns into the output file that do not fall in any of the column
ranges defined with the sk parameter.

See also: esort, emerge, eselect 
##esort
esort: The esort command can be used to sort big data files that do not
fit into main memory. The syntax is:

    esort(      
        df=...,       name of output file (required)    
        sk=...,       up to five sort keys   
        noc=...,      number of records in temp. file, def. 1000 
        len=...,      maximal record length, def. 1000 
    ) = input_file;           
                
The command expects the name of a standard ASCII data file on its 
right-hand side. This file must have valid end-of-record characters.
The maximal record length can be specified with the len parameter.
Also required is the name of an output file to be given with the df
parameter. Finally, at least one sort key must be defined with the 
sk parameter. The syntax is:

    sk = i1,j1,i2,j2,...    

where the right-hand side is a sequence of up to five pairs of column
numbers. Each pair specifies one sort key. The first sort key is taken
to be the field from column i1 up to, and including, column j1; and
so on. If there are two or more sort keys they are used hierarchically
from left to right.

See also: eselect, eskip, emerge
##evalf  
evalf: The evalf command can be used to evaluate the value of a function.
The syntax is:

    evalf(     
        fmt=...,         print format 
        argument1=...,   value of first argument, def. 0  
        argument2=...,   value of second argument, def. 0  
        ...,             and so on  
    ) = function;             

Using evalf1, instead of evalf, one also gets first derivatives (if
possible); evalf2 tries to calculate first and second derivatives.

##evalfi 
evalfi: The evalfi command can be used to evaluate the value of an         
inclusion function. The syntax is:

    evalfi(     
        arg1 = l1,u1,    domain for argument, def. [0,0]
        fmt=...,         print format, def. 12.4
    ) = interval_function;             

arg1 must be substituted by the argument name used in the function
definition. If there is more than one argument, the arg parameter can be
repeated.

Using evalfi1, instead of evalfi, one also gets an inclusion function
for first derivatives.

##exists
exists: Type 1 operator

    exists(Name)

where Name is a syntactically valid matrix, variable, or namelist name.
The operator returns 1 if the object denoted by Name exists, otherwise
it returns 0. In particular,

    exists()

returns 0. This is useful in order to check whether $n expressions in macro
definitions are actually available when the macro is executed. For example,          

    exists($1)

when used inside a macro, will be expanded to
                                     
    exists(Name)    

when Name is given for $1 when invoking the macro, and is expanded into

    exists()
                                     
when the macro argument $1 is not used when invoking the macro. Remember
that $n expressions are substituted by their corresponding names, when given
by the user, or substituted by Null-strings. This rule also applies when
$n expressions are used inside double quotation marks. The only exception
is when $n expressions are inside single quotation marks. For example,

    print("$1 ...");

will substitute $1 by a valid name, or by the Null-string; but

    print("... `$1' ...")

will not substitute $1 by a name or the Null-string.
##factor analysis
factor analysis. See: dma

##fmin
fmin: The fmin command can be used to find a local minimum of a function.
The syntax is:

    fmin(     
        mina=...,     minimization algorithm, def. 5   
        dopt=...,     1 for numerical approx of derivatives, def. 0    
        dscal=...,    scaling factor for function, def. 1    
        mxit=...,     max number of iterations  
        mxitl=...,    max number of line search iterations, def. 50 
        crit=         convergence criterion  
        tolg=...,     tolerance: norm of gradient, def. 10e-6   
        tolf=...,     tolerance: change of function value, def. 10e-12   
        tolp=...,     tolerance: change of parameters, def. 10e-4   
        tolv=...,     tolerance: variance of function values, def. 10e-10   
        tols=...,     tolerance: reduction factor, def. 10e-4   
        tolsg=...,    tolerance: scaled gradient, def. 10e-5   
        tolsp=...,    tolerance: scaled parameter change, def. 10e-8   
        slen=...,     step length, def. 1 
        sred=...,     reduction factor, def. 0.5 
        smin=...,     minimum of step size, def. 10e-10   
        sst=...,      option for evaluation of derivatives, def. 0 
        prot=...,     (or prot1) name of protocol file 
        pfmt=...,     print format for protocol file, def. -19.11 
        xp=...,       starting values 
        dsv=...,      input file with starting values 
        ppar=...,     output file with estimated parameters 
        tfmt=...,     print format for parameters, def. 10.4 
        ccov=...,     type of covariance matrix 
        pcov=...,     output file with covariance matrix 
        mfmt=...,     print format for covariance matrix, def. 12.4 
        pres=...,     additional output file 
        mfmt=...,     print format for pres option, def. 12.4 
        v=...,        varlist for pres option           
        mplog=...,    write function value into matrix
        mppar=...,    write parameters into matrix
        mpcov=...,    write covariance matrix into matrix
        mpgrad=...,   write gradients into matrix
    ) = function;    

See also: fml, mina, mpcov, mpgrad, mplog, mppar, prot
##fml 
fml: The fml command is for maximum likelihood estimation using a
user-defined log-likelihood function. The syntax is identical with 
the fmin command. The only difference is the default scaling factor,      
being -1 for the fml, and 1 for the fmin command.

See: fmin
##freg 
freg: The freg command is for nonlinear regression using a user-defined
regression function. The syntax is identical with the fmin command.
For further information see Section 6.16.1 of the Manual.

See: fmin
##freq
freq: The freq command can be used to create joint frequency tables for
a set of variables. The syntax is:

    freq(     
        maxcat=...,   maximum number of categories, def. 1000   
        fmt=...,      print format, def. 3.0    
        tfmt=...,     print format for contingency measures, def. 12.4 
        df=...,       output file
    ) = varlist;               

Note that the command assumes that all variables have integer values.

See also: freq1, freq2, loglin
##freq1
freq1: The freq1 command has syntax:

    freq1(     
        maxcat=...,   maximum number of categories, def. 1000   
        fmt=...,      print format, def. 3.0    
        tfmt=...,     print format for contingency measures, def. 12.4 
        df=...,       output file
    ) = varlist;               

It creates a separate frequency tables for each variable contained in
varlist. Note that the command assumes that all variables have integer
values.

See also: freq, freq2, loglin
##freq2
freq2: The freq2 has syntax:

    freq2(     
        maxcat=...,   maximum number of categories, def. 1000   
        fmt=...,      print format, def. 3.0    
        sc=1,         contingency measures 
        tfmt=...,     print format for contingency measures, def. 12.4 
        df=...,       output file
    ) = varlist;               

varlist must contain exactly two variables which are assumed to have
integer values. The command creates a two-dimensional frequency table    
and, optionally, calculates contingency measures.

See also: freq, freq1, loglin
##frml 
frml: The frml command is for maximum likelihood estimation of user-
defined transition rate models. The syntax is:

    frml (parameters) = loglikelihood_function;

Parameters are identical to those that can be used with the fmin
command. The default scaling factor is -1 (same as for the fml 
command).

See: fmin
##gap 
gap: The gap command solves the following assignment problem: Given an
n x n matrix D, find a permutation of column indices, say p(i), such that
the sum of matrix elements, D(i,p(i)), becomes minimal. 

    gap(     
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1    
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
        df=...,     write permuted matrix  
    ) = fname;           
      
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The command requires as input a graph defined with
the gdd command. It is expected that edge values are integral. In any
case, edge values will be casted into integer values without warning.
By default, the command only writes the optimal permutation vector into
its standard output file. As an option, one can use the df parameter
to request an output file containing the permuted matrix.
##gbcf
gbcf: Given a directed valued graph, the gbcf command provides information
about direct and indirect backward control.

    gbcf(     
        gn=...,    graph number, def. 1 
        if=...,    input file containing node numbers (optional)
        sc=...,    minimum level of influence, def. 0.5 
        opt=...,   output option, def. 1
        nfmt=...,  integer print format, def. 4 
        fmt=...,   print format for values, def. 10.4
        df=...,    optional second output file 
        ns=...,    max number of controlled nodes
    ) = fname;          

##gcd
gcd: The gcd command can be used to create data files containing unvalued
graphs with a specified number of nodes and edges. The syntax is:

    gcd(
        opt=...,    option, def. 1 
                    1 = complete graph, without loops 
                    2 = complete graph, including loops
                    3 = randomly select m edges 
        n=...,      number of nodes, def. 10
        m=...,      number of edges, def. 1
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;

Except for the name of an output file to be given on the right-hand side,
all parameters are optional. The data are written to the output file in
form of an edge list. Each record contains values of three variables,
I, J, and V. The first two variables contain the node numbers, the third
variable, V, has value 1 or -1. If V(i) = 1, there is an edge from 
I(i) to $J(i); otherwise there is no edge.
##gcliq
gcliq: Given an undirected graph, the gcliq command finds all of its
cliques (maximally connected subgraphs).

    gcliq(    
        alg=...,    algorithm, def. 1   
                    1 = Bron-Kerbosch
                    2 = Harary-Ross 
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1 
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
        min=...,    minimum size of cliques, def. 3
        sort,       sort node numbers
        df=...,     second output file
    ) = fname;           

All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
an undirected graph.

Given the df option, the command will create a second output file organized
as a node list. Each record will contain three entries:
    
column 1 : node number                                        
column 2 : number of component the node belongs to            
column 3 : number of clique the node belongs to               

##gcon
gcon: Connected components in a graph.

    gcon(
        opt=...,    option, def. 1 
                    1 = node list 
                    2 = edge list 
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;
      
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. By default, the command uses the first graph (gn=1)
in the currently defined multigraph. The contents of the output file
depends on the opt parameter. By default (opt=1), the command creates a
node list. The first column of the output file numbers the connected
components, then follows the number of nodes in the component, and 
finally the internal and external node numbers. If opt=2, the data for
each component are written as an edge list.
##gcset
gcset. The gcset command finds compact sets in an undirected valued graph.

    gcset(     
        opt=...,    output option, def. 1   
                    1 = one record for each compact set
                    2 = one record for each node 
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1  
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
        fmt=...,    print format for flow values, def. 10.4
        df=...,     print pseudo image graph 
    ) = fname;           
      
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
an undirected valued graph. Loops are ignored.
##gcut
gcut: The gcut command finds cut nodes in an undirected graph.

    gcut(      
        opt=...,     option, def. 1 
                     1 = one record for each component
                     2 = one record for each node  
        gn=...,      graph number, def. 1    
        nfmt=...,    integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;

All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. 
##gcyc
gcyc: Given an undirected graph, the gcyc command can be used to calculate
a fundamental set of cycles and, optionally, all cycles.

    gcyc(
        opt=...,     option, def. 1   
                     1 = fundamental cycles, node list
                     2 = fundamental cycles, edge list
                     3 = all cycles, version 1  
                     4 = all cycles, version 2  
        gn=...,      graph number, def. 1    
        nfmt=...,    integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;           
     
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. 
##gda
gda: The gda command can be used to aggregate a valued (directed or
undirected graph.         

    gda(
        rcn=...,        recode string
        nfmt =...,      print format for integers, def. 4   
        fmt = ...,      print format for values, def. 10.4  
    ) = fname;          output file name

The recode string has the following syntax:

    rcn = n1[k11,k12,...],n2[k21,k22,...],...

meaning that nodes k11, k12,... are aggregated into the new node number n1,
k21, k22,... are aggregated into the new node number n2, and so on.
Part of the string may look as follows:

    ni[ki1,,ki2,...]

Then nodes numbers from ki1 to ki2 are aggregated into the new node  
number ni.

The aggegated graph is written to the output file specified on the
right-hand side, always as an edge list.
##gdcon
gdcon: Given a directed graph, the gdcon command finds, for each node i,
the set of other nodes that can be reached by a directed path from i.
The graph must be directed, but can be defined with any of the options
provided by the gdd command. 

    gdcon(
        opt=...,    option, def. 1 
                    1 = number of reachable nodes 
                    2 = plus list of node numbers 
                    3 = strong components, node list
                    4 = strong components, edge list
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1 
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4 
        fmt=...,    print format for edge values, def. 10.4
        if=...,     input file with nodes numbers 
    ) = fname;

All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. By default, the command uses the first graph
(gn=1) in the currently defined multigraph. If the if parameter is not
used, the command performs its calculations for all nodes in the input
graph. Alternatively, one can specify the name of an input file with
the if parameter. The first numerical entry in each of its records is
then interpreted as a node number, and the command only considers these
node numbers.

The output file will contain  one record for each node number that has
an out-degree of at least 1. The first two columns show, respectively, the
internal and external node number. The third column contains the number of
nodes which are reachable by a directed path. If opt=2, further columns
show the numbers of the nodes that are reachable.

If opt = 3 or opt = 4, the command creates an output file that provides
information about the strong components of the graph. It will be a 
node list if opt = 3, an edge list if opt = 4. In both cases, the if
parameter will be ignored.
##gdcyc
gdcyc: The gdcyc command enumerates cycles in a directed graph.
The graph must be defined with gdd option 1 (edge list).

    gdcyc(    
        opt=...,     output option, def. 1  
                     1 = one record for each cycle
                     2 = one record for each node 
                     3 = nothing written; instead, the progress in the
                         number of cycles is reported in standard output
        dopt=...,    option for termination, def. 0
                     if 1, the command terminates after the first
                     cycle has been found 
        gn=...,      graph number, def. 1    
        nfmt=...,    integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;           
      
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. 

##gdd
gdd: The gdd command is used to define a graph data structure. Syntax is

    gdd(
        opt=...,    option, def. 1
                    1 = edge list 
                    2 = pointer to adjacency matrix  
                    3 = lower triangle               
                    4 = upper triangle               
                    5 = lower triangle, including diagonal
                    6 = upper triangle, including diagonal
                    7 = full adjacency matrix (variables)
                    8 = full adjacency matrix (matrices)
        gt=...,     graph type      
                    1 = undirected, unvalued
                    2 = undirected, valued 
                    3 = directed, unvalued 
                    4 = directed, valued 
        perm=...,   if perm=1 the gdd data structure is permanently
                    saved, def. perm=0
        sc=...,     minimal level of valid edges, def. 0
                    (must be non-negative)
    ) = varlist;

If opt=1 (edge list), varlist can be specified as follows:

gdd = I,J,V;    The node set will be created as the set of all integers
                that occur as values of variables I or J. Each record
                defines an edge (possibly a loop). Values of V are       
                taken to be edge values. The graph is directed and
                valued.

gdd = I,J,V1,V2,...;

                This defines a multigraph. It will consist of a single
                node set defined by variables I and J, but a different
                valued graph is created for each variable V1,V2,...

gdd = off;      deletes a currently defined graph data structure.

A relational data structure defined with the gdd command remains available
for subsequent commands until one of the following conditions occurs:

-   The data structure is explicitly removed with: gdd = off;        
-   The gdd command is used again to define a new gdd data structure,
-   At least one of the variables used in the currently defined gdd
    data structure is no longer available (only if perm=0).
-   The number of cases in the data matrix is changed with a tsel 
    command (only if perm=0).

If used without arguments, the gdd command provides information about currently 
defined relational data.

##gde    
gde: The gde command can be used to create an undirected (multi-) graph from 
a list of node numbers and object IDs.                    
                                                                        
    gde(                                                      
      df = ...,           output file (required)            
      ni = ...,           if 1 do not write loops, def. 0  
      nfmt = ...,         integer print format, def. 4      
    ) = N,ID1,ID2,...;                                        
                                                                        
The command requires at least two variables on the right-hand side. N must
contain positive integers interpreted as a list of node numbers.   
ID1, ID2, ... can be integer or floating point. Non-negative values   
of these variables are interpreted as IDs of properties of the nodes given 
by N. (Negative values are ignored.)                  
                                                                        
For each ID variable the command constructs an undirected graph. The node
set contains all different node numbers found in variable N.                                          
                                                                        
For each ID variable, the value of an edge (i,j) is calculated as the 
number of times that an object with the same property (identified by ID) 
belongs both to node i and node j.                                            
                                                                        
The resulting (multi-) graph is written to the output file specified with
the df parameter as an edge list. If ni = 1, loops are not written to the
output file.                                       
##gdln
gdln: The gdln command provides information about forward and backward
links in directed graphs. The syntax is:

    gdln(
        opt=...,    option, def. 1 
                    1 = forward links
                    2 = backward links 
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1 
    ) = fname;

##gdp
gdp: The gdp command can be used to write graph data into an output
file. The syntax is:

    gdp(      
        opt=...,    print option, def. 1   
                    1 = edge list   
                    2 = lower triangle of adjacency matrix   
                    3 = same as 2 but including the diagonal   
                    4 = complete adjacency matrix   
                    5 = adjacency matrix written as a square matrix   
        gn=...,     graph number   
        sc=...,     substitute for missing edges, def. -1
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4           
        fmt=...,    print format for values, def. 10.4            
        if=...,     input file containing node numbers          
    ) = fname;           

##gdu
gdu: The gdp command can be used to create the union of two graphs or
multigraphs.

    gdu(                                                       
        df=...,         output file                            
        sc=...,         substitute for missing values, def. -1 
        nfmt=...,       integer print format, def. 4             
        fmt=...,        print format for values, def. 10.4     
    ) = I,J,V1,...;                                            
                                                                           
The command assumes that a graph (possibly a multigraph), called graph 1,
is already defined with the perm option. In addition, the command expects
on its right-hand side at least threee variables from the current data
matrix that can be interpreted as an edge list defining a second graph, 
called graph 2. Interpretation is in the usual way: The first two variables,
I an J, provide node numbers that must be positive integers. Each additional
variable specifies the edge values of a graph. In this way, also graph 2 can
be a multigraph.             
                                                                         
The command creates a new graph consisting of all nodes numbers and edges
that are part of graph 1 or graph 2. The new graph is written as an edge
list to the output file specified with the df parameter. If one of the graphs
contains isolated nodes without valid edge values they are written at the end
of the output file.

The output file begins with two columns containing the node numbers. Then
follow the edge values of the subgraphs from graph 1, then the edge values
of the subgraphs from graph 2.
##gep
gep: The gep command can be used to find all paths connecting any two nodes
of a graph and to determine their minimal (or maximal) length and/or value.
The graph can be directed or undirected but must be defined with gdd
option 1 (edge list).

    gep(
        opt=...,   output option, def. 1 
                   1 = one record for each pair of nodes
                   2 = all paths                        
                   3 = distance matrix, minimal values 
                   4 = distance matrix, maximal values 
                   5 = location of nodes in paths, version 1
                   6 = location of nodes in paths, version 2
        max=...,   max number of paths (opt 6), def. 100 
        gn=...,    graph number, def. 1 
        nfmt=...,  integer print format, def. 4
        fmt=...,   print format for values, def. 10.4
        if=...,    input file for node selection  
        ns=...,    max edges in input file, def. 100 
    ) = fname;

Except for the name of an output file on the right-hand side all parameters
are optional. By default, the command considers all pairs of nodes in the
given graph. Optionally, one can specify a subset of nodes with the if
parameter. If a file is specified with this parameter, the command tries to 
interpret the first two integer entries in each of its records as external
node numbers i and j, respectively, and then only considers paths from i   
to j. If this option is used, the command needs to know a maximal number
of records (pairs of nodes) from the input file. This can be specified with
the ns parameter; default is a maximum of 100 pairs of nodes.
##gev
gev: The gev command can be used to calculate a selected number of
eigenvalues and eigenvectors of  the adjacency matrix of an undirected
graph. 

    gev(     
        n=...,      number of eigenvalues/vectors, def. 1
        eps=...,    required accuracy, def. 1.e-6  
        mxit=...,   max number of iterations, def. 30 
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1    
        fmt=...,    print format, def. 10.4
        prot=...,   protocol file
    ) = fname;
     
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
an undirected graph. The n parameter specifies how many eigenvalues/vectors
will be calculated. The command tries to find those eigenvalues, and
corresponding eigenvectors, whose absolute value is largest. the maximal
number of eigenvalues/vectors that can be requested is N - 1, where N is
the number of nodes in the graph.

The calculated eigenvalues are shown in the standard output, the
corresponding eigenvectors are written into the output file.
##gfc
gfc: Given a directed valued graph, the gfc command provides information
that can be used to calculate measures of flow control.

    gfc(    
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
        fmt=...,    print format for flow values, def. 10.4
    ) = fname;           

All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
a directed valued graph that must be defined with option 1 of the gdd 
command, i.e. as an edge list.      
##gfcf
gfcf: Given a directed valued graph, the gfcf command provides information
about direct and indirect forward control.

    gfcf(     
        gn=...,    graph number, def. 1 
        sc=...,    minimum level of influence, def. 0.5
        opt=...,   output option, def. 1 
                   1 = information for all nodes 
                   2 = information only for controlled nodes 
        nfmt=...,  integer print format, def. 4 
        fmt=...,   print format for values, def. 10.4 
        df=...,    second output file
        ptab=...,  third output file
        if=...,    input file containing node numbers (optional)
    ) = fname;          

##gflow
gflow: Given a directed graph, the gflow command finds a maximal flow
for each pair of nodes that is connected by at least one directed path.
If the graph is unvalued, it is assumed that all edges have value 1.

    gflow(     
        opt=...,    output option, def. 1   
                    1 = one record per flow 
                    2 = square flow matrix  
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1    
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
        fmt=...,    print format for flow values, def. 10.4
    ) = fname;           

All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
a directed graph that must be defined with option 1 of the gdd command,
i.e. as an edge list.
##gio
gio: Given a directed valued graph, the gio command provides information
about integrated ownership.

    gio(     
        gn=...,    graph number, def. 1           
        if=...,    input file containing node numbers   
        mxit=...,  maximum number of iterations, def. 20   
        eps=...,   epsilon for convergence, def. 0.0001    
        opt=...,   output option, def. 1 \\
                   1 = print all y(ij) values   
                   2 = print only values where
                       a(ij) >= s  or  y(ij) >= s.      
        sc=...,    s value for opt=2, def. 0.0     
        nfmt=...,  integer print format, def. 4    
        fmt=...,   print format for values, def. 10.4 
    ) = fname;          

##giset
giset: The giset command tries to find a maximal independent set in an
undirected graph, based on a greedy randomized search procedure (GRASP).

    giset(     
        mxit=...,    max number of iterations, def. 100 
        idf=...,     values for alpha and beta, def. 0.1,0.1
        seed=...,    seed of random number generator, def. 270001  
        min=...,     minimal size of independent set               
        gn=...,      graph number, def. 1    
        cg=...,      1 to use complementary graph,  def. 0    
        nfmt=...,    integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;           
      
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
an undirected graph. By default, the algorithm tries to find a maximum
independent set and uses the maximum number of iterations specified with
the mxit parameter. The best independent set found in these iterations is
finally reported and written into the output file. If the min parameter
is used to specify a required minimum size for the independent set, 
iterations are stopped as soon as an independent set having the required
size is found. If silent = -1, the command reports about its iterations
on the standard error output.
##gmin
gmin: This is an experimental command for global function minimization
based on interval calculations. The syntax is:

    gmin(                                                   
        ns=...,         controls usage of derivatives, def. 0
                        0  command will not use derivatives               
                        1  command will use derivatives     
        xp=...,         starting values plus boxes          
        dsv=...,        starting values provided by a file  
        mxit=...,       max number of iterations, def. 100  
        nbox=...,       max number of boxes, def. 100       
        tolbw=...,      tolerance box width, def. 1.e-4     
        tolfd=...,      tolerance for function range, 1e-10 
        tolfe=...,      for global min/max, def. 1.e-10     
        fmt=...,        print format, def. 13.6             
        prot=...,       protocol file                       
    ) = function;                                           

##gmst
gmst: Given an undirected valued graph, the gmst command calculates a
minimum, or maximum, spanning tree for each of its connected components.

    gmst(
        alg=...,     algorithm, def. 1  
                     1 = Kruskal    
                     2 = Prim       
        max=...,     1 if maximum spanning tree, def. 0
        gn=...,      graph number, def. 1 
        sort,        sort wrt node numbers
        nfmt=...,    integer print format, def. 4
        fmt=...,     print format for values, def. 10.4
    ) = fname;           

All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
an undirected and valued graph. By default, the command uses the first
graph number and calculates a minimum spanning tree. The max=1 parameter
can be used to request a maximum spanning tree.
##gnc
gnc: Node-centered networks. The command
                                                                        
    gnc(                                                      
      gn = ...,       graph number, def. 1                  
      nfmt = ...,     integer format, def. 4                
      fmt=...,        print format for values, def. 10.4    
    ) = fname;        output file (required)                  
                                                                        
assumes an undirected graph. The output file will contain one record for
each node of the graph with the following entries:         
                                                                        
Col 1: internal node number                                        
Col 2: external node number                                        
Col 3: ni = degree of node i plus 1 (= number of nodes in the      
       node-centered network of node i)                            
Col 4: ki = number of edges in the node-centered network           
Col 5: density, calculated as di = (2 ki)/(ni (ni - 1))            
##gnst
gnst: Given an undirected graph, the gnst command enumerates all of its
spanning trees. 

    gnst(
        opt=...,    output option, def. 1   
                    1 = one record for each tree 
                    2 = edge list   
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1 
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;           
     
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. 
##gni
gni: The gni command can be used to get information about the nodes of
a currently defined graph data structure. The syntax is

    gni(
        gn=...,     graph number
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;

The result is written into the output file fname. The graph is always
interpreted as a directed unvalued graph.
##gqap
gqap: The gqap command tries to solve a quadratic assignment problem.     

    gqap(     
        alg=...,     algorithm, def. 1    
                     1 = approximative solution   
        mxit=...,    max number of iterations, def. 100 
        seed=...,    seed of random number generator, def. 270001
        idf=...,     specification of alpha and beta, def. idf=0.25,0.5
        gn=...,      graph number for D and F      
        df=...,      write permuted matrix            
        nfmt=...,    integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;           
      
The command expects two integer-valued graphs, D and F, whose graph numbers
must be specified with the gn parameter. By default, the command uses the
first two graphs of the current multigraph. The algorithm always performs 
the maximum number of iterations specified with the mxit parameter. 
