##grp
grp: Some commands allow to use the grp parameter for a specification of
groups of cases. The syntax is

    grp = G1,G2,...,

where G1,G2,... are variables. Each variable defines one group consisting
of those cases where the variable has a nonzero value.
##gsort
gsort: Topological sorting of a directed graph.

    gsort(
        opt=...,   option, def. 1 
                   1 = list of old/new labels
                   2 = graph with new labels
        gn=...,    graph number, def. 1    
        nfmt=...,  integer print format, def. 4
        fmt=...,   print format for values, def. 10.4
    ) = fname;           

All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
a directed graph that is defined by option 1 of the gdd command, i.e.,
by an edge list. If the algorithm finds a cycle it stops with an error
message.
##gsp
gsp: The gsp command finds, for each pair of nodes in a graph, a shortest
path. The graph can be directed or undirected, valued or unvalued, but must
be defined with gdd option 1 (edge list). If the graph is unvalued, all
edge values are assumed to be 1.

    gsp(
        opt=...,     option, def. 1   
                     1 = only reachable nodes 
                     2 = square matrix with leading columns 
                     3 = square matrix without leading columns  
        gn=...,      graph number, def. 1  
        nfmt=...,    integer print format, def. 4
        fmt=...,     print format for values, def. 10.4
    ) = fname;           

All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. 
##gst
gst: Given an undirected graph, the gst command finds a spanning tree
for each connected component of the graph. 

    gst(
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1    
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
        fmt=...,    print format for values, def. 10.4
    ) = fname;           
     
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
an undirected graph. The output file has four columns. The first column
contains an index number for the tree. The other three columns show the
edges of the tree (node numbers and edge values).
##gsym
gsym: Given n directed graph, the gsym command checks whether, for each
edge from node i to node j, there also is an edge from node j to node
i with the same edge value. If this is the case the command reports
that the graph is symmetrical. Syntax:

    gsym(gn=...);

gn can be used to specify a graph number, by default gn = 1.
##gtcl
gtcl: The gtcl command finds the transitive closure of a graph.

    gtcl(     
        gn=...,   graph number, def. 1 
        nfmt=..., integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;          
     
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. By default, the command uses the first graph
(gn=1) in the currently defined multigraph. The output file contains
the adjacency matrix plus two leading columns containing, respectively,
the internal and external node numbers.
##hcld
hcld: Hierarchical divisive clustering.                       

    hcld(     
        alg=...,    algorithm, def. 1 
                    1 = max different cluster centers
                    2 = partition with minimal diameter
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1
        min=...,    minimal cluster size (for alg=1), def. 2
        max=...,    maximal number of splits (for alg=2), def. 1    
        df=...,     output file with clustering hierarchy    
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4    
        fmt=...,    print format for values, def. 10.4 
    ) = fname;          

The command requires an undirected valued graph. All parameters except for
the name of an output file on the right-hand side are optional.
##hcls
hcls: command for SAHN clustering procedures. The command requires a
complete dissimilarity matrix without missing values and needs storage for
the lower triangle of this matrix.

    hcls(     
        opt=...,   option, def. 1 
                   1 = single-link         
                   2 = complete-link       
                   3 = weighted average    
                   4 = weighted centroid   
                   5 = group average       
                   6 = unweighted centroid 
                   7 = Ward's minimum variance  
        gn=...,    graph number, def. 1         
        ptab=...,  print implied distance matrix                    
        nfmt=...,  integer print format, def. 4 
        fmt=...,   print format for values, def. 10.4
        df=...,    output file with dendrogram       
        pcf=...,   plot file for dendrogram          
        np=...,    if np=1 use/add external node numbers, def. 0
    ) = fname;          

All parameters except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side are optional. This output file will contain information about
the hierarchical merging of objects into clusters. Optionally one can
request two more output files. Using the df parameter will create an output
file that shows the dendrogram as a standard edge list. The pcf parameter
creates another output file that can be used as a TDA command file to 
create a PostScript plot of the dendrogram. 

Note that the np=1 option assumes that the graph is defined with gdd 
option 1 or option 2 (edge list) in order to effectively distinguish 
between internal and external node numbers.

See also: hclsp
##hclsp
hsclp: Having created a dendrogram with the hcls command, one can use     
the hclsp command to create arbitrary partitions of the objects (leafs of
the tree). The syntax is:
                                                                        
    hclsp(                                                    
        nlev=...,   number of levels, def. 0                   
        cn=...,     sequence of node numbers                  
        nfmt = ..., integer print format, def. 4              
    ) = output_file;                                          
                                                                        
The command requires a graph defined with the gdd command, option 1 (edge
list). As an edge list one can use the output file created by the df option
of the hcls command. In any case, the resulting graph must be a tree 
with a single root node defined by an outdegee 0. There are two opions
that can be used separately or simultaneously:

(1) If nlev > 0, the command determines the root node, that is, the   
    single node with outdegree 0 and reconstructs the tree for nlev     
    levels, beginning from the root node. The truncated tree is written
    to the output file and shows, in addition to the node numbers, the   
    number of leafs that have a directed path to the node. This option
    might be helpful in selecting a suitable set of internal nodes of
    the tree to define a partition of the leafs.
                                                                        
(2) The second option requires the specification of a sequence of     
    nodes (external node numbers) with the parameter                  
                                                                          
        cn = n1,n2,...,                                               
                                                                        
The output file will then contain one record for each leaf of the tree  
that has a path to one of the nodes n1,n2,... Assuming that node i has 
a path to node ni, the record will contain two entries: first ni, then i.
                                                                        
Note that is is not required that the nodes n1,n2,... define a complete
partition of the leafs. If a leaf has two or more nodes from the set 
n1,n2,... as followers, the assignment is arbitrary. 
                                                                        
See also: hcls.
##help
help: The help command provides information about entries in the help 
file, "tda.hlp". This file must be available in one of the directories
containing executable files. The syntax is:

    help string;    

with an arbitrary string which may contain wild cards (* and/or ?). The
string is NOT case sensitive, multiple blanks are reduced to a single
blank character. If a single key word matches string, the corresponding
entry is shown in the standard output. Otherwise one gets a list of all
matching key words. In addition, one can use:

    help;           shows the entry for the help key word.
    help .;         repeats the most recently used help command.

The help command waits for a user input after 24 lines have been written
to the screen. Input is interpreted as follows:

    next line      just a return 
    next page      any character (except q and -) followed by return
    previous page  - character followed by return
    quit           q[uit] followed by return
##histogram
histogram: See the following commands:

    atab, ploth

##iddf
iddf: This command calculates the distribution function of a set-valued
discrete variable. The syntax is:

    iddf(                                               
        opt=...,        option, def. 1
                        1 calculate min, max, and mean distribution funktion
                        2 calculate also a self-consistent distr function
        mxit=...,       max number of iterations, def. 50         
        tolf=...,       tolerance, def. 1e-3            
        df=...,         optional output file            
        prot=...,       protocol file                   
    ) = XL,XH;          lower and upper bound of set-valued variable
                                                        
See also: sddf, idf
##idf
idf: This command calculates the distribution function of an interval-valued
variable. The syntax is:

    idf(                                               
        fmt = ...,      print format, def. 13.6
    ) = XL,XH;          lower and upper bound of interval-valued variable
                                                        
See also: iddf, sddf      
##ierr
ierr: By default, the program terminates when a command returns an error. 
If the command

    ierr;

is given in the command line when invoking the program the return status
of commands will be ignored.
##if     
if: The if/endif pair of commands can be used for conditional command
execution. The syntax is:

    if (expression);
        ...
    endif;

The commnds in between if and endif are executed if expression is true
(see the explanation given for the while command). Alternatively, one can
use the syntax:                                   

    if (expression);
        ...
    else;
        ...
    endif;

if/endif pairs can be nested up to 100 levels and can be used inside of
repeat/endrepeat and while/endwhile loops.

See also: break, repeat, while 
##imean
imean: This command calculates the mean of an interval-valued variable.
The syntax is:

    imean(                                             
        fmt = ...,      print format, def. 13.6
    ) = XL,XH;          lower and upper bound of interval-valued variable
                                                        
##ineq
ineq: The ineq command can be used to calculate inequality measures.
The syntax is:
                                                      
    ineq(     
        fmt=...,      print format, def. 10.4    
        df=...,       output file 
        mppar=...,    write parameters into matrix
    ) = varlist;               

See also: mppar
##int
int: The command

    int(
        ab=a,b,     integration interval, no default    
        fmt=,       print format, def. fmt=0.0            
    ) = function;     

can be used to find the integral of function for the interval defined
by the ab parameter. The command uses the currently selected integration
method, see niset.
##interaction effect
Interaction effects: See ndvar.
##iterative proportional fitting
Iterative proportional fitting: See mpfit.
##ivar
ivar: This is an experimental command intended to calculate the variance
of an interval-valued variable. The syntax is:

    ivar(                                                   
        mxit=...,       max number of iterations, def. 100  
        nbox=...,       max number of boxes, def. 100       
        tolbw=...,      tolerance box width, def. 1.e-4     
        tolfd=...,      tolerance for function range, 1e-6  
        tolfe=...,      for global min/max, def. 1.e-6      
        fmt=...,        print format, def. 13.6             
    ) = XL,XH;          lower and upper bounds of interval-valued variable

See also: imean
##lcnt
lcnt: The command

    lcnt (noc=...) = fname;

writes a frequency distribution of the lengths of the records in the file
fname into standard output. By default, a maximum of 1000 records is read
from the file. This can be changed with the optional noc parameter.

Note that record length is without end-of-record characters.
##life table
life table: See: ltb

##limitations
limitations: Some of TDA's limitations can be changed with the following
commands.

    maxnv=...     maximum number of variables, def. 2000     
    maxmat=...    maximum number of matrices, def. 200      
    maxstd=...    stack size for derivatives, def. 20       
    cblen=...     command buffer length, def. 20000    

These commands can only be used in the command line when invoking the
program.
##local
local: The command

    local (A,B,C,...);

where A,B,C,... are matrix names, can be used inside of macro definitions.
The command declares the specified matrix names as being only locally
defined. Then, using these names for matrix operations inside the macro 
will not conflict with identical names that are already defined outside 
the macro. The command is ignored if used outside of a macro definition.

##logit
logit: See: qreg

##loglin
loglin: The loglin command can be used to create contingency tables and to
estimate loglinear models. The syntax is:

    loglin(                                                             
        maxcat=...,             max categories per dimension, def. 1000        
        w = ...,                variable containing weights             
        scale = ...,            variable for scaling                    
        ptab=...,               output file for table                   
        nfmt=...,               print format for table values, def. 2        
        mod=...,                model descriptions                       
        mxit=...,               max number of iterations, def. 20       
        tolf=...,               convergence tolerance, def. 1e-8        
        tfmt=...,               print format for parameters, def. 10.4       
        ppar=...,               output file for parameter estimates     
        pres=...,               output file for fitted table and residuals
        pcov=...,               output file for covariance matrix                       
        mfmt=...,               print format for cov matrix, def. 12.4      
        screen,                 screening marginal and partial associations
    ) = X1,...,Xn;              list of variables                       

All parameters except for a list of variables on the right-hand side are
optional. A maximum of 26 variables can be specified on the right-hand
side (corrsponding to the letters A,...,Z). It is assumed that these    
variables are integer-valued. The command creates a contingency table
from these variables. Optionally, the variable defined with the w parameter
is used for weights. 

If requested, the table is written into an output file as specified with
the ptab parameter.

The parameter screen can be used to request a screening of the table
for all marginal and partial associations.

The mod parameter can be used to request estimation of loglinear models.
The syntax is:

    mod = model_description,

How to define models is explained in the Manual, Section 6.19.2. The mod
parameter can be used up to 100 times.
##lsreg
lsreg: The lsreg command performs least squares regression, optionally
with constraints. The syntax is:

    lsreg(    
        w=...,          case weight variable, def. no weigths
        ni=1,           if without intercept, def. ni=0
        lsecon=...,     equality constraints
        lsicon=...,     inequality constraints
        dgrp=...,       estimate dummy variables with constraints
        tfmt=...,       print format for results, def. tfmt=10.4
        s=1,            use robust covariance matrix, def. s=0
        df=...,         print data to an output file 
        fmt=...,        print format for df option, def. 10.4
        dtda=...,       TDA description file for df option 
        ppar=...,       print estimated coefficients to output file
        pcov=...,       print covariance matrix to output file
        pres=...,       print residuals to output file 
        mfmt=...,       print format for pcov and pres option
        mplog=...,      write norm of residuals into matrix
        mppar=...,      write parameters into matrix
        mpcov=...,      write covariance matrix into matrix
    ) = varlist;

varlist is a comma-separated list of variables. The first variable in
varlist is interpreted as the dependent variable.

See also: l1reg, mpcov, mplog, mppar
##l1reg
l1reg: The l1reg command performs L1-norm regression.
The syntax is:

    l1reg(
        ni=1,         if without intercept, def. ni=0
        tfmt=...,     print format for results, def. 10.4 
        df=...,       print data to an output file 
        fmt=...,      print format for df option, def. 10.0
        ppar=...,     print estimated coefficients to output file
        pcov=...,     print unscaled covariance matrix to output file
        pres=...,     print residuals to output file
        mfmt=...,     print format for pcov and pres option, def. 12.6
        mplog=...,    write norm of residuals into matrix
        mppar=...,    write parameters into matrix
        mpcov=...,    write covariance matrix into matrix
    ) = varlist;

varlist is a comma-separated list of variables. The first variable in
varlist is interpreted as the dependent variable.

See also: lsreg, mpcov, mplog, mppar
##loop commands
loop commands: The following commands can be used to program loops and
control structures:

    break
    if/endif
    repeat/endrepeat
    while/endwhile

See: break, if, repeat, while.

##ltb
ltb: The ltb command can be used to calculate life tables.
The syntax is:

    ltb(    
        tp=...,         time periods, no default   
        cfrac=...,      fraction of censored cases that are counted   
                        as part of the risk set, def. 0.5 
        grp=...,        group indicator variables, def. without groups
        fmt=...,        print format for output file, def. fmt=7.5
        ap=1,           append data to end of output file  
    ) = name_of_output_file;     

The command requires a definition of episode data with the edef command.

See also: edef, grp, time periods, ple
##mach
mach: The command

    mach;

without arguments provides information about machine parameters.
##macro
macro: There are three commands for macros:

    macrodef        defines a new macro
    macrolist       shows currently defined macros
    macroclear      deletes all currently defined macros

##macroclear
macroclear. The command

    macroclear;

without arguments deletes all currently defined macros.
##macrodef
macrodef: A macro can be defined with the command

    macrodef(MName) = {string1; string2; ... };

MName is an arbitrary string to be used as the name of the macro.
string1, string2, etc. must be valid TDA commands, or already defined
macro names. MName then becomes a new command, and

    MName;

is executed as

    string1;
    string2;
    ...

string1, string2,... may contain variable arguments, having the 
predefined names

    $1, $2, ..., $100

The maximal number of aruments equals the maximal number of macros and
is currently set to 100.
           
When defining a macro, variable arguments must be contiguous, that is,
$1,$2,$3,... Apart from this requirement, they can be used in any way.
When invoking the macro name with arguments, TDA uses the following
ordering:

    MName(arg_n+1,arg_n+2,... ) = arg_1,arg_2,...,arg_n;

arg_i is then substitued for $i in the macro definition. Arguments can
be skipped by using consecutive commas. The corresponding argument in
the macro definition will then be empty.

In order to check the expansion of macros one can use the command

    expand = MName (arguments) = arguments;

This will show the expanded macro based on the provided arguments but
will not execute the commands.

See also: macroclear, macrolist, local
##macrolist
macrolist: The command

    macrolist;

without arguments shows a list of all curently defined macros.
##mag
mag: The command

    mag(A,R,C,B)

expects an (n,m)-matrix A, a (n,1)-vector R and a (1,m)-vector C. Elements
of R correspond to the rows of A, elements of C correspond to columns of A.
If these elements are zero the corresponding rows and/or columns of A are
dropped. If two or more elements of R and C have the same value, the
corresponding elements of A are aggregated.

Example:

                1 2 3       1                       
            A = 5 6 7   R = 0  C = 1 4 4   B = 2 8
                1 2 1       1                       

##matrix commands
Matrix commands                                                     section 
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- 
mag         aggregation of matrix elements
maxmat      sets maximum number of matrices
mcath       horizontal concatenation
mcatv       vertical concatenation
mcathv      horizontal and vertical concatenation (direct sum)
mcel        creates an edge list from a matrix
mcent       centering
mchol       Cholesky decomposition
mcross      cross product of a matrix    
mcsum       column sums of a matrix
mcvec       stacks columns of a matrix into a vector
mdcent      double centering
mdcol       creates diagonal matrix from column sums
mdef        creates a new matrix (several options)   
mdefb       creates a new matrix containing a data matrix block
mdefc       creates a matrix with constant values
mdeff       creates a matrix from a data file
mdefi       creates an identity matrix
mdefg       creates an adjacency matrix
mdiag       creates diagonal matrix from vector
mdiagd      extracts main diagonal into vector
mdrow       creates diagonal matrix from row sums
mev         eigenvalues and vectors
mevs        eigenvalues and vectors, symmetric matrix
mexpr       evaluates a matrix expression 
mexpr1      evaluates a matrix expression in block mode
mfmt        sets print format
mfree       free matrix allocation 
mginv       generalized inverse matrix
minvd       inverse matrix from main diagonal
minvs       inverse matrix from lower triangle
mivec       inverse of vec operator (mcvec)
mkp         Kronecker product
mlp         linear programming
mlp1        linear programming with equality constraints
mls         least squares regression
mlse        linear equations
mlsi        linear inequalities
mlsei       least squares regression with constraints
mmul        multiplication of matrices
mnc         create a new matrix depending on some level
mncol       provides number of columns
mnls        least squares regression with constraints
mnorm       largest absolute value
mnorm1      L1 norm
mnorm2      Euclidean norm
mnrow       provides number of rows
mnum        creates sequence of numbers
mnvar       creates a new internal data matrix from given matrix
mpbl        permutation to lower block diagonal form
mpbu        permutation to upper block diagonal form
mpcol       column permutation of a matrix
mpfit       iterative proportional fitting
mpinv       inverse permutation vector
mpit        iteration of Leslie matrix
mple        product-limit estimation (Kaplan-Meier)
mpr         prints a matrix expression
mpra        append to file
mproc       Procrustes rotation
mprow       row permutation of a matrix 
mpsym       symmetrical permutation of a matrix
mpz         permutation for zero-free diagonal
mqap        quadratic assignment problem
mrank       provides rank values
mrsum       row sums of a matrix
mrvec       stacks rows of a matrix into a vector
mscal1      scales a matrix
mscol       selects columns from a matrix
msetv       set matrix elements to specified values
msort       sorts a vector
msort1      sorts a vector, suppresses double entries
msqrtd      square root from main diagonal
msqrti      inverse square root from main diagonal
msrow       selects rows from a matrix
mstand      standardization
msvd        singular value decomposition
msvd1       singular value decomposition
mtrace      calculates trace
mtransp     matrix transposition
mtrim       delete or add rows and columns
mwvec       calculation of weights
mwvec1      calculatoin of weights

For most matrix commands also separate entries are available. For general
information about matrices see: matrix expression.
##matrix expression
matrix expression: The concept of matrix expression generalizes standard
TDA expressions. Matrix expressions have basically the same syntax as
standard expressions but may contain matrix names instead of numerical
constants.
          
1.  Matrices are kept as named numerical objects in TDA. Conventions for
    names are the same as for variables. Values are always stored in
    double precision. By default, the maximum number of matrices is 200.
    This value can be changed with the maxmat command.                   
                        
    The mdef command shows the currently defined matrices.

2.  Matrix expressions.

a)  Matrix expressions are evaluated element-wise by their corresponding
    standard expressions. A standard expression containing only numerical
    constants and operators is equivalent to a (1,1) matrix expression.
    For example, with matrices

                1
            A = 2   and B = (1,2)
                3

        the command

            mexpr(A * B,R);
                                          1  2
        would result in the matrix:  R =  2  4
                                          3  6

b)  A matrix-expression can combine matrices, data matrix variables
    and namelists. Assume

        mexpr(op(A,...,V,...,L,...), R)

    where A,... are matrices, V,... are data matrix variables, and
    L,... are namelists. Data matrix variables are treated as NOC x 1
    matrices, namelists are treated as NOC x m matrices where m is the
    number of variables contained in the namelist. Consequently, all
    arguments can be considered as matrices with dimensions, say,
    row(i) x col(i). The resulting matrix, R, gets dimension 

        max {row(i)} x  max {col(i)}

    If one of the matrices (or variables, namelists) has smaller dimensions,
    its missing rows, or columns, are created by cyclically using the
    available ones. For example, if a matrix A has n columns, its (n+1)th
    column will equal its first column, and so on; analogously for rows.

c)  As a consequence, it is no longer possible to create matrices having
    names that conflict with already defined variables or namelists.

d)  Matrix-expression may contain type 2 operators. Not allowed are the
    pre() and suc() operators (but the lag operator will work), and the
    specific operators for episode and sequence data.

    When a matrix-expression contains type 2 operators, the resulting matrix
    is calculated as a sequence of column vectors, meaning that the 
    operators apply separately for each of these column vectors. For example,

        mexpr(sort(X),R);

    would separately sort each column of X in ascending order.

e)  In order to allow the block mode operators for matrix expressions,
    there is an additional command,

        mexpr1(B,m-expr,R);

    where B is a (m,n) matrix and m-expr is some (row,col) matrix
    expression. It is required that m >= row. The resulting matrix, R,
    has dimension  max(m,row) x col and is evaluated from m-expr.
    While evaluating m-expr, B is used for the definition of blocks,
    that is, each consecutive number of identical rows in B is
    treated as a separate block.

f)  In addition to its standard form, one can use the mexpr command
    in the form

        mexpr(<rsel,csel,a>, expression, Matrix-name)

    The optional string <rsel,csel,a> controls how the matrix expression is
    evaluated. rsel selects row indices, csel selects column indices of the
    matrix expression. Only elements selected both by rsel and csel are 
    evaluated via expression, all other elements get a value defined by a.

    a can be a scalar constant or the name of an already existing matrix.
    rsel and csel can be specified as follows:

            *             selects all row (column) indices
            i             selects row (column) index i
           -i             selects all rows (columns) except i
           (i1,i2,...)    selects rows (columns) i1,i2,...
          -(i1,i2,...)    selects all rows (columns) expept i1,i2,...
            i(d)j         selects rows (columns) i, i+d, ...    
          -(i(d)j)        selects all rows (columns) except
                          i, i+d, i+2d,... 

g)  A matrix-expression may contain arguments

        X(row,col)          where X is a matrix, or
        V(row,1)            where V is a data matrix variable, or
        L(row,col)          where L is a namelist.

    The result is the corresponding element of X, V, or L. For example

        mexpr(A(row,col),R);

    would create a scalar matrix, R, containing the value of
    A(row,col). row and col can be any scalar expression.

    Values resulting from row and col expressions must be positive
    integers. If they exceed the corresponding matrix dimensions, they
    are cyclically turned around. For example, if the matrix is

        A = (1,2,3),

    then A(1,5) = 2.

    In addition, one can use the following constructs (where X is
    a matrix, a variable, or a namelist):

        X(row,.)        sum of elements in row             
        X(.,col)        sum of elements in column
        X(.,.)          sum of all elements

    All constructs mentioned so far are considered as type 1 operators
    and get the dimension 1 x 1. There is a further type 2 operator,

        X(i)

    which returns the i.th column of the matrix (or variable, or
    namelist) X. The returned object has dimension row x 1 where
    row is the number of rows of object X.

h)  Whenever the argument of a matrix command refers to an existing
    matrix, it is possible to use an arbitrary matrix-expression
    instead of a single matrix name. So it is possible to combine
    scalars, matrices, variables and namelists in all matrix commands.
    For example,

        mpr(sin(V));

    where V is a data matrix variable would print the resulting vector.

i)  Vectors can be defined using the syntax:

        < e1,e2,... >               for column vectors
        < e1,e2,... >'              for row vectors

    where e1, e2, ... are any scalar expressions. For example,      
    always treated as column vectors. For example,

        mpr(<1,2,3>');

    would be printed as the row vector

        1 2 3 

    and

        mpr(<1,2,3> + <4,5,6>' );
   
    would be printed as the matrix          

        5 6 7
        6 7 8
        7 8 9

    If a vector has fewer elements as required by the dimension of its
    context, it is cyclically updated. For example,
             
        mpr(<1,2>' + <3,4,5>')

    would result in the vector

        4 6 6 

    Note 1: The prime character (') is different from the single
    quotation mark character (`) which can be used to enclose strings.

    Note 2: The prime character can only be used with
    vectors, not with general matrices or matrix-expressions.

    Note 3: Due to stack size limitations, a directly specified
    vector can have at most 9 elements. This may be less when the
    vector is used as part of a more complex expression.
##maxmat
maxmat: The command     

    maxmat=...

can be used to change the maximum number of matrices, default is 200.
The command can only be used in the command line when invoking the program.

See also: limitations
##maxnv
maxnv: The command     

    maxnv=...

can be used to change the maximum number of variables, default is 2000.
The command can only be used in the command line when invoking the program.

See also: limitations
##maxstd
maxstd: The command     

    maxstd=...

can be used to change the stack size for evaluating derivatives, default
is 20. The command can only be used in the command line when invoking
the program.

See also: limitations
##mbrr
mbrr: See brr
##mcath
mcath: Given matrices A1,A2,..., the command

    mcath(A1,A2,...,R);   

creates a new matrix R containing the horizontal concatenation of   
A1,A2,..., i.e. R = [A1,A2,...].
##mcathv
mcathv: Given matrices A1, A2,...,An, the command

    mcathv(A1,A2,...,An,R);   

creates a new matrix R containing the direct sum of A1, A2,..., An,
definded as:

         |A1        |
         |  A2      |
     R = |     .    |
         |      .   |
         |       An |

See also: mcath, mcatv.
##mcatv
mcatv: Given matrices A1,A2,..., the command

    mcatv(A1,A2,...,R);   

creates a new matrix R containing the vertical concatenation of   
A1,A2,...
##mcel
mcel: Given a (n,n)-matrix A and a scalar x, the command

    mcel(A,x,L)

creates a (m,3)-matrix L organized as an edge list, m is the number of
valid edges in A. A coefficient a(i,j) represents a valid edge if
a(i,j) >= x.

##mcent
mcent: Given a matrix A, the command

    mcent(A,R);   

creates a new matrix R and sets the columns of R to the mean-centered
values of the columns of A.        

See also: mstand
##mchol
mchol: Given a positive definite (n,n) matrix A, the command

    mchol(A,R);   

returns the Cholesky decomposition of A in R. TDA does not check whether
A is symmetric, it simply uses the lower triangular part of A to perform     
the decomposition. The result is then copied into the lower triangular part
of the (n,n) matrix R. If A is not positive definite, there will be an
error message and R = 0.
##mcross
mcross: The command

    mcross(A,B);   

creates a new matrix B that will be the cross product of A and the
transpose of A.
##mcsum
mcsum: Given an (n,m)-matrix A, the command

    mcsum(A,C)       

creates a (1,m)-vector C, with C(j) = A(1,j) + ... + A(n,j).     

See also: mrsum
##mcvec
mcvec: Given an (n,m)-matrix A, the command

    mcvec(A,V)       

creates a (n * m,1)-vector V containing the stacked columns of A.

See also: mrvec
##mdcent
mdcent: Given an (n,n) matrix A, the command

    mdcent(A,R);   

creates an (n,n) matrix R that contains the double-centered values of A.
##mdcol
mdcol: The command

    mdcol(A,C);   

assumes that A is a (m,n) matrix. The command creates a (m,m)        
diagonal matrix C and sets

    C[j,j] = sum{i=1,m} A[i,j]             

##mdef  
mdef: The command
       
    mdef;

without arguments shows the currently defined matrices. The same command
can be used to define new matrices.

    mdef(A);            creates a new (m,n) matrix that contains the
                        current data matrix. m is the number of cases,
                        and n the number of variables in the current
                        data matrix.

    mdef(A,m,n);        creates a new (m,n) matrix consisting of the
                        first m cases and n variables in the current
                        data matrix.

    mdef(A) = varlist;  creates a new (m,n) matrix consisting of the
                        variables specified on the right-hand side.
                        m is the number of cases in the current data
                        matrix, n is the number of variables in varlist.

    mdef(A,m,n)=fname;  creates a new (m,n) matrix; values are taken
                        from the first n entries in the records of the
                        data file fname. A maximum of m records is 
                        read from the data file. See also: mdeff.

    mdef(A,m,n) = a11,a12,...,a1n,
                  a21,a22,...,a2n,
                  am1,am2,...,amn;

                        creates a new (m,n) matrix by using the values
                        given on the right-hand side.
##mdefb
mdefb: The command

    mdefb(A,expr)        

creates a new matrix, A, that contains one block of the current data
matrix. The command requires that block mode is defined with the dblock
command and that expr evaluates to a valid block number (between 1 and
the maximal number of blocks). Given that expr evaluates to the integer
number n, block number n is copied into the matrix A.
##mdefc
mdefc: The command

    mdefc(m,n,d,A)        

creates a (m,n) matrix A and sets all elements to the constant value d,
assumed to be an integer or floating point number.
##mdeff
mdeff: Given that fname is the name of a standard free-format data file with
m data records and n numerical entries in each record, the command

    mdeff(X) = fname;

creates an (m,n) matrix, X, containing these values.
##mdefg
mdefg: Given a relational data structure, one can use the mdefg command
to create a matrix containing a graph's adjacency matrix. The syntax is:

    mdefg(
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1
        sc=...,     substitute for missing edges, def. -1
    ) = matrix_name;           
      
Except for the name of a matrix to be given on the right-hand side
all parameters are optional. By default, the command uses the first
graph from the current relational data structure. The sc parameter
can be used to substitute specific values for missing edges.
##mdefi
mdefi: The command

    mdefi(m,n,A);    

creates a (m,n) matrix A and sets A(i,i) = 1 for i=1,...,min(m,n).
All other elements of A are set to zero.
##mdiag
mdiag: The command

    mdiag(A,D);   

assumes that A is a (m,1) matrix (column vector). The command creates
a (m,m) matrix D and copies the elements of the main diagonal of A 
into the main diagonal of D.              
##mdiagd
mdiagd: The command

    mdiagd(A,D)   

where A is a (m,n) matrix, creates a (k,1) column vector with 
k = min(m,n) containing the main diagonal elements of A.
##mdrow
mdrow: The command

    mdrow(A,R);   

assumes that A is a (m,n) matrix. The command creates a (m,m)        
diagonal matrix R and sets

    R[i,i] = sum{j=1,n} A[i,j]             

##mem
mem: The command

    mem;

without arguments shows the current memory usage.
##mev   
mev: The command

    mev(A,ER,EI,EVR,EVI)

expects a real (m,m) matrix A. The command creates (m,1) vectors ER and EI
containing, respectively, the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvalues 
of A. It also creates the (m,m) matrices EVR and EVI containing,respectively,
the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvectors. The eigenvector for 
the i.th eigenvalue is stored in the i.th columns of EVR and EVI.
##mevs  
mevs: The command

    mevs(A,E,V)

expects a symmetric (m,m) matrix A. The command creates (m,1) vectors E
containing the eigenvalues of A, and a (m,m) matrix V containing the
eigenvectors of A, stored in the columns of V.
##mexpr
mexpr: The command
       
    mexpr(expression,A);

where expression is a matrix expression. This command creates a new 
matrix A, with dimensions defined by expression and each element set
to the corresponding value of expression.

In addition to its standard form, one can use the mexpr command in
the form

    mexpr(<rsel,csel,u>, expression, A)

The optional string <rsel,csel,u> controls how the matrix expression is
evaluated. rsel selects row indices, csel selects column indices of the
matrix expression. Only elements selected both by rsel and csel are
evaluated via expression, all other elements get a value defined by u.

u can be a scalar constant or the name of an already existing
matrix. rsel and csel can be specified as follows:

    *             selects all row (column) indices
    i             selects row (column) index i
   -i             selects all rows (columns) except i
   (i1,i2,...)    selects rows (columns) i1,i2,...
  -(i1,i2,...)    selects all rows (columns) expept i1,i2,...
    i(d)j         selects rows (columns) i,i+d,...    
  -(i(d)j)        selects all rows (columns) except i,i+d,i+2d,... 

For example, mexpr(<1,*,A>,log(A),B) creates a matrix B that has in
its first column the logarithm of the corresponding elements of matrix
A and otherwise the original values of A.

In order to allow the block mode operators for matrix expressions, there
is an additional command,

    mexpr1(B,m-expr,R);

where B is a (m,n) matrix and m-expr is some (row,col) matrix expression.
It is required that m >= row. The resulting matrix, R, has dimension
max(m,row) x col and is evaluated from m-expr. While evaluating m-expr,
B is used for the definition of blocks, that is, each consecutive number
of identical rows in B is treated as a separate block.

See also: matrix expression.        
##mfmt
mfmt: The command

    mfmt = print_format;

specifies a new print format for matrices. Default is mfmt=10.4.
##mfree
mfree: The command

    mfree(A);

deletes the matrix A. The command

    mfree;

without arguments deletes all currently defined matrices.
##mginv 
mginv: Given a (m,n) matrix A, with m >= n, the command

    mginv(A,B)

returns the generalized inverse of A in the (n,m) matrix B.
##mina
mina: The mina parameter can be used to select one of the available
algorithms for function minimization.

    1   Direct search   
    2   Simplex algorithm   
    3   Method of conjugate gradients   
    4   BFGS algorithm      
    5   Newton algorithm (I)   
    6   Newton algorithm (II)   
    7   CES (quadratic model)   
    8   CES (tensor model)    

See also: fmin
##minvd  
minvd: The command
       
    minvd(A,B);

given a (m,n) matrix A, returns a (m,n) matrix B with

    B(i,i) = 1 / A(i,i) for i = 1,...,min(m,n).
                                               
All other elements are set to zero.
##minvs  
minvs: The command
       
    minvs(A,B);

given a positive definite symmetric (m,m) matrix A, returns the inverse
of A. Values are taken from the lower triangle of A.
##mivec
mivec: The command

    mivec(V,n,A)

performs the inverse of the vec operation. Given a q-vector V and a
positive integer n with n * m = q (for some m), the command creates a 
(n,m)-matrix A. The first n elements of V are stored in the first column
of A, the following n elements are stored in the second column, and so on.
##mkp
mkp: Given matrices A1 and A2, the command

    mkp(A1,A2,R);   

creates a new matrix R containing the Kronecker product of A1 and A2.
##mlp
mlp: The command

    mlp(T,X,Y); 

solves a linear programming problem for the data given in the tableau T.
The command

    mlp1(T,p,X,Y);  
                    
does the same but interprets p rows of T as equality constraints.
##mls  
mls: The command

    mls(S,B);   

tries to find a least squares solution of

    norm (Y - X * B) -> minimal

Y is assumed to be a (m,1) vector, X a (m,n) matrix. The input data must
be given as a (m,n + 1) matrix

    S = [X,Y].

The command returns the (n,1) vector B. The residual norm and the rank of
X are written into the standard output. The contents of S is destroyed
by the command.
##mlse 
mlse: The command

    mlse(S,B);   

tries to find a solution of

    X * B = Y                     

Y is assumed to be a (m,1) vector, X a (m,n) matrix. The input data must
be given as a (m,n + 1) matrix

    S = [X,Y].

The command returns the (n,1) vector B. If m > n the command returns a
least squares solution. If there is more than one solution, the command
returns one of the solutions. The residual norm and the rank of X is
written into the standard output. The contents of S is destroyed by
the command.
##mlsei
mlsei: The command
                   
    mlsei(W,me,mi,X);

expects a (m,n+1) matrix W and two scalar expressions, me and mi,
in the following way:

        ( E F )
    W = ( A B )
        ( H G )

where: E is a (me,n) matrix
       F is a (me,1) vector
       A is a (ma,n) matrix
       B is a (ma,1) vector
       H is a (mi,n) matrix
       G is a (mi,1) vector

and ma = m - me - mi. Note: m is defined by the number of rows of
the matrix W, me and mi are given as scalar parameter in the command.
It is possible to have me >= 0, ma >= 0, and mi >= 0.
The command tries to find an (n,1) vector X such that:

    E X  = F     (equality constraints)
    H X >= G     (inequality constraints)

and the norm of (AX - B) is minimal in the least squares sense.
The algorithm is the same as used for TDA's lsreg command.
##mlsi 
mlsi: The command

    mlsi(S,B);   

tries to find a solution of

    X * B >= Y                     

Y is assumed to be a (m,1) vector, X a (m,n) matrix. The input data must
be given as a (m,n + 1) matrix

    S = [X,Y].

The command returns the (n,1) vector B. Note that a solution might not
exist. In any case, the contents of S is destroyed by the command.     
##mdsc
mdsc: Multidimensional scaling with principal coordinates. Syntax:
                                                            
    mdsc(                                                 
      gn=...,     graph number, def. 1                    
      opt=        method if negative eigenvalues, def. 1   
                   1 = do nothing                          
                   2 = add constant to distance matrix     
      df=...,     additional output file                  
      fmt=...,    print format, def. 10.4                 
    ) = fname;                                            

The command requires an undirected valued graph (dissimilarity matrix D).
It then constructs from D a doubly centered matrix A and performs
a spectral decomposition A = R L R', with L and R containing, respectively,
the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A. If requested with the df parameter,
the A matrix, eigenvalues and eigenvectors are written into an output
file.       

Coordinates corresponding to positive eigenvalues are written into
the output file specified at the right-hand side of the command. 

##mmul
mmul: The command

    mmul(A,B,R);   

performs the operation R = A * B; a new (m,n) matrix R, with m equal
to the number of rows of A and n equal to the number of columns of B,
is created and filled with the matrix product of A and B. The operation
requires that the number of columns of A is equal to the number of
rows of B.
              
The mmul command can be used in a more general way as

    mmul(A1,...,Ak,R);  

This performs the operation R = A1 * A2 *...* Ak.
##mnc
mnc: The command

    mnc(A,x,opt,B)                                  

expects a (n,m)-matrix A, a scalar x and a scalar opt. The command then
creates a new (n,m)-matrix B that has the following values:
                                                            
    if opt = 1:   bij = 0 if aij <= x        
    if opt = 2:   bij = 0 if aij <  x        
    if opt = 3:   bij = 0 if aij >= x        
    if opt = 4:   bij = 0 if aij >  x                            
    if opt = 5:   bij = 0 if abs(aij) <= x                       
    if opt = 6:   bij = 0 if abs(aij) <  x                       
    if opt = 7:   bij = 0 if abs(aij) >= x                       
    if opt = 8:   bij = 0 if abs(aij) >  x                       
                                                            
otherwise bij = aij.
##mncol
mncol: Given an m x n matrix A, the command

    mncol(A,C);

creates a 1 x 1 matrix C containing the number of columns in A, that is, n.
##mnls
mnls: The command
                   
    mnls(W,me,k,X);

expects a (m,n+1) matrix W and two scalar expressions, me and k,
in the following way:

    W = ( E F )
        ( A B )

where: E is a (me,n) matrix
       F is a (me,1) vector
       A is a (ma,n) matrix
       B is a (ma,1) vector

and ma = m - me. Note: m is defined by the number of rows of
the matrix W, me is given as a scalar parameter in the command
and it is possible that me >=0 and ma >= 0.

The command tries to find an (n,1) vector X such that EX = F
(equality constraints) and the norm of (AX - B) is minimal in 
the least squares sense. In addition, k is a scalar parameter
with 0 <= k <= n and is used to specify non-negativity conditions
for the solution vector X:  

    X(k) >= 0, X(k+1) >= 0,..., X(n) >= 0

If k = n there are no non-negativity constraints; if k = 0 all
components of X should be non-negative.
##mnorm
mnorm: Given a matrix A, the command

    mnorm(A,R);

returns the maximal norm of A, that is, the absolute value of the matrix
element that has largest absolute value. The resulting matrix, R, has
dimension 1 x 1.

    mnorm1(A,R);

returns the L1 norm of A, that is, the sum of the absolute values of the
matrix elements.

    mnorm2(A,R);

returns the L2 (Euclidean) norm of A.
##mnrow
mnrow: Given an m x n matrix A, the command

    mnrow(A,C);

creates a 1 x 1 matrix C containing the number of rows in A, that is, m.
##mnum
mnum: Given values x, d, and n, the command 

    mnum(x,d,n,A);

creates a n x 1 matrix A with values

    A(i) = x + (i - 1) * d          (for i = 1,...,n)

x, d, and n can be 1 x 1 matrices.
##mnvar
mnvar: The command
       
    mnvar(A);

where A is the name of a (m,n) matrix, creates a new data matrix with
m cases and n variables. Variable names are created by using the matrix
name and adding column numbers.
##moving average
moving average: see: sma
##mpbl   
mpbl: Permutation to lower block-diagonal form.

    mpbl(A,B,P,N,U)

The command expects an (n,n)-matrix A and tries to find a simultaneous
permutation of rows and columns to make A a lower block-diagonal matrix.
The permuted matrix is returned in B. Als returned is an (n,1)-vector P
that contains the permutation, an (1,1)-scalar N that contains the number
of blocks, and a (k,1)-vector U with k the number of blocks. U(i,1) is the
number of the row where the i.th block begins.

The algorithm is adapted from ACM algorithm 529 (Permutations to block
triangular form), developed by I.S. Duff and J.K. Reid.

See also: mpbu
##mpbu   
mpbu: Permutation to upper block-diagonal form.

    mpbu(A,B,P,N,U)

The command expects an (n,n)-matrix A and tries to find a simultaneous
permutation of rows and columns to make A an upper block-diagonal matrix.
The permuted matrix is returned in B. Als returned is an (n,1)-vector P
that contains the permutation, an (1,1)-scalar N that contains the number
of blocks, and a (k,1)-vector U with k the number of blocks. U(i,1) is the
number of the row where the i.th block begins.

The algorithm is adapted from ACM algorithm 529 (Permutations to block
triangular form), developed by I.S. Duff and J.K. Reid.

See also: mpbl
##mpcol 
mpcol: column permutation of a matrix.

    mpcol(A,P,B)

A is (n,m)-matrix, P is (m,1) or (1,m) permutation vector. The command
creates an (n,m) matrix B with B(i,j) = A(i,P(j)).

See also: mprow, mpsym.
##mpcov
mpcov: Some commands support the mpcov parameter with syntax

    mpcov = Matrixname;

If used, the parameter creates a new matrix having the specified name
and copies the estimated covariance matrix into the new matrix.
##mpfit
mpfit: Iterative proportional fitting. The comand

    mpfit(A,U,V,iter,eps,B)

expects a (n,m)-matrix A, a (1,m)-vector U containing the prescribed column
sums, a (n,1)-vector V containing the prescribed row sums, a scalar iter that
specifies the maximum number of iterations, and a scalar eps that specifies
the required accuracy. The matrix A must be non-negative and all row and
column sums must be positive. Also the following equality should hold:
Sum U[j] = Sum V[i]. Otherwise the command will issue a warnings message
(but will not terminate).

The command creates a (n,m)-matrix B that contains the estimates with the
prescribed row and column sums.
##mpinv
mpinv: Given an (n,1) or (1,n) permutation vector P, the command

    mpinv(P,Q)

creates the vector Q with Q(P(i)) = i.
##mpit
mpit: The command

    mpit(F,N,k,R);

can be used to iterate a Leslie matrix. It is assumed that F is a (n,2) 
matrix, N is a (n,1) vector, and k is an integer. The first column of F 
contains the first row of the (n,n) Leslie matrix L, and the second column
of F contains the subdiagonal of L (the last element is ignored). The vector N 
contains the starting values for the iteration. The command creates the 
(k + 1,n) matrix R. The i.th row of R contains L^i N.

##mpgrad
mpgrad: Some commands support the mpgrad parameter with syntax

    mppar(v=varlist) = Matrixname;

If used, the parameter creates a new matrix having the specified name
and writes the final case-specific gradients into the matrix. If the
optional v parameter is used, case-specific values of the variables in
varlist are added to each row of the matrix.
##mple   
mple: The command
       
    mple(T,C,F,D);

given (m,1) vectors T and C, performs a product-limit estimation of the
cumulative distribution function of the values in T. The elements in C
are interpreted as censoring information: if C(i) = 0, T(i) is interpreted
as NOT censored; if C(i) has a nonzero value, the corresponding T(i) is
interpreted as censored. The largest value of T is always interpreted as
NOT censored. The command creates two (m,1) vectors F and D. If the command
terminates successfully, F will contain the cdf, and D the jumps of the cdf
for noncensored values.

See also: ple
##mplog
mplog: Some commands support the mplog parameter with syntax

    mplog = Matrixname;

If used, the parameter creates a new (1,1) matrix having the specified
name. The value written into the matrix depends on the command. In
general, commands using ML estimation write the final value of the
log-likelihood into the matrix. The lsreg command writes the final norm
of the least squares residuals, the l1reg command writes the final sum
of absolute deviations.
##mppar
mppar: Some commands support the mppar parameter with syntax

    mppar = Matrixname;

If used, the parameter creates a new matrix having the specified name
and writes parameter values resulting from the command into that matrix.
The values written into the matrix depend on the command. In general,
commands that estimate a model write the estimated model parameters 
into the matrix.
##mpr
mpr: The command

    mpr(matrix_epression [, string] ) [= filename];

prints the matrix expression into standard output using the currently active
print format. As an option, one can use a string that is then printed in
ahead of the matrix expression. If the name of an output file is specified
on the right-hand side, values of the matrix expression are written into
that file. Any previously existing file with the same name is deleted.
If one uses mpra, instead of mpr, the new values are appended to an
already existing file.

See also: mfmt
##mproc
mproc: Matrix command for Procrustes rotation.

    mproc(X,Y,Y1);

X and Y must refer to already defined n x m matrices. The command returns
an optimally transformed n x m matrix Y1. If silent = -1, the scaling factor,
the translation vector, and the rotation matrix will be written into the
standard output.
##mprow
mprow: row permutation of a matrix.

    mprow(A,P,B)

A is (n,m)-matrix, P is (n,1) or (1,n) permutation vector. The command
creates an (n,m) matrix B with B(i,j) = A(P(i),j).

See also: mpcol, mpsym.
##mpsym
mpsym: symmetrical permutation of a matrix.

    mpysm(A,P,B)

A is (n,n)-matrix, P is (n,1) or (1,n) permutation vector. The command
creates an (n,n)-matrix B with B(i,j) = A(P(i),P(j)).

See also: mprow, mpcol.
##mpz
mpz: Given an (n,n)-matrix A, the command

    mpz(A,B,P)

tries to find a permutation of the rows of A that puts as many non-zero
elements in the main diagonal as possible. The permuted matrix is returned
in B. Also returned is an (n,1)-vector P that contains the permutation.
##mqap
mqap: approximate solution of the quadratic assignment problem based on
algorithm CACM 608 (D. H. West).

    mqap(F,D,C,P)

The command expects (n,n)-matrices F, D and C (n > 1) and tries to find
a permutation p that minimizes

    sum_i ( c[i,p(i)] + sum_j f[i,j] d[p(i),p(j()] )                            

The best permutation is returned in the (n,1)-vector P. The best function
value is written to standard output.                    

Note: The algorithm expects that the main diagonals of F and D are zero.
If the mqap command finds non-zero elements they are set to zero. The
number of changes is reported in the standard output.
##mrank
mrank: See msort
##mrsum
mrsum: Given an (n,m)-matrix A, the command

    mrsum(A,R)       

creates a (n,1)-vector R, with R(i) = A(i,1) + ... + A(i,m).  

See also: mcsum
##mrvec
mcvec: Given an (n,m)-matrix A, the command

    mrvec(A,V)       

creates a (n * m,1)-vector V containing the stacked rows of A.

See also: mcvec
##mscal1
mscal1: The command
       
    mscal1(A,B);
    
given a (m,n) matrix A, returns a (m,n) matrix B with values

    B(i,j) = A(i,j) / Sum(A)      

where Sum(A) is the sum of all values of A.
##mscol
mscol: There are two commands that can be used to select columns, or rows,
from a matrix  
      
    mscol(A,D,R);       select columns
    msrow(A,D,R);       select rows

In these commands, A is a matrix, D is a row or column vector that provides
indices of the rows (in msrow) or columns (in mscol) that are to be
selected. This vector can be given by a matrix name or directly with the
vector notation. For example, if

                    1 2 3
                A = 4 5 6
                    7 8 9

the command

                mscol(A,<1,3,2>,R)

would result in

                    1 3 2  
                R = 4 6 5  
                    7 9 8  
      
It is possible to use expressions of the form <i1,,i2,...>. This is expanded
to the range of indices from i1 to i2. 
##msetv
msetv: The command sets specific values of a matrix. Syntax is:

    msetv(expr, X(row,col));

where expr is a scalar expression, X is the name of an existing matrix, and
row and col are scalar expression referring to an existing element of X.
The command copies the value of expr into X(row,col).
##msort
msort: sorting and ranking.  

    msort (A,D,R);
    msort1(A,D,R);
    mrank (A,D,R);

A is a general (m,n) matrix, D is a column vector providing indices of
columns of A. The matrix A is then sorted in ascending order, based on
the columns selected by D.

The msort command returns a (m,n) matrix containing the sorted values of A.
The msort1 command does the same but drops rows that occur more than once.
The mrank command returns a (m,1) vector containing rank numbers for the
sorted rows of A.
##msqrtd
msqrtd: The command
    
    msqrtd(A,R);  

assumes that A is a (m,m) square matrix with non-negative entries in
its main diagonal. The command creates a (m,m) diagonal matrix R and
sets
        R(i,i) = sqrt( A(i,i) )           

##msqrti
msqrti: The command

    msqrti(A,R);  

assumes that A is a (m,m) square matrix with non-negative entries in
its main diagonal. The command creates a (m,m) diagonal matrix R and
sets
        R(i,i) = 1 / sqrt( A(i,i) )            

##msrow
msrow: See mscol.
##mstand
mstand: Given a matrix A, the command

    mstand(A,R);   

creates a new matrix R and sets the columns of R to the standardized 
values of the columns of A.        

See also: mcent.
##msvd  
msvd: Given a (m,n) matrix A, with m >= n, the command

    msvd(A,Q)

performs a singular value decomposition of A. The singular values are
returned in the (n,1) vector Q. Alternatively, one can use the command

    msvd1(A,Q,U,V)

that performs a singular value decomposition               

    A = U Q transp(V)
                                            
and returns the singular values in the (n,1) vector Q and also returns
the orthogonal transformation matrices U and V.
##mtrace
mtrace: Given an (m,n) matrix A, the command

    mtrace(A,R);

returns the trace of A, i.e.,

    R = sum {A(i,i)}    for i = 1,...,min(m,n)

##mtransp
mtransp: Given an (m,n) matrix A, the command

    mtransp(A,B);  

creates the (n,m) matrix B containing the transpose of A.
##mtrim
mtrim: The matrix command         

    mtrim(A,ca,ra,cb,rb,R)

expects a (n,m)-matrix A and scalar expressions ca, ra, cb, and rb.
It creates a new matrix, R, by deleting the first ca columns of A 
(if ca >= 0) or adding ca zero columns (if ca < 0), and correspondingly 
treats the first ra rows of A, the last cb columns of A, and the last
rb rows of A.

See also: msrow, mscol
        
##multidimensional scaling
Multidimensional scaling: See mdsc, mdsm, mdsn

##multinomial logit
multinomial logit: See: qreg

##multiple variables
multiple variables: Inside the nvar command, multiple variables can be
defined as follows:

    VNAME{j,q,k}<s>[n.m](label) = expression,

where the storage size <s>, the format definition [n.m], and the label
(label) are optional. j, q, and k must be integers with 0 <= j <= q, 
and k >= 1. The definition is then expanded into q - j + 1 variable 
definitions as follows:

    VNAMEj   <s>[n.m](label) = expression(k),
    VNAMEj+1 <s>[n.m](label) = expression(k+1),
    VNAMEj+2 <s>[n.m](label) = expression(k+2),
    ...
    VNAMEq   <s>[n.m](label) = expression(k+q-1),

where expression(k+l) means that all ck terms in expression are substituted
by ck+l terms.

Example 1:

    Y{0,3,2} = c2,

is expanded into

    Y0 = c2,
    Y1 = c3,
    Y2 = c4,
    Y3 = c5,

Example 2:

    Y{1,4,3} = sqrt(c3) + sqrt(c5),

is expanded into

    Y1 = sqrt(c3) + sqrt(c5),
    Y2 = sqrt(c4) + sqrt(c5),
    Y3 = sqrt(c5) + sqrt(c5),
    Y4 = sqrt(c6) + sqrt(c5),

##multivariate probit
multivariate probit: See: qreg
##mwvec  
mwvec: The command
       
    mwvec(A,W,R);

given (m,1) vectors A and W, creates a (m,1) vector R, with elements

              Sum[j in I(i)] A(i) * W(i)
    R(i) =  ------------------------------
                  Sum[j in I(i)] W(i)

The sum runs over all j in the index set

    I(i) := { k : k > i }

R(i) = A(i) if I(i) empty, or the sum of weights is zero.
##mwvec1 
mwvec1: The command
       
    mwvec1(A,W,T,R);

given (m,1) vectors A, W, and T, creates a (m,1) vector R with elements

              Sum[j in I(i)] A(i) * W(i)
    R(i) =  ------------------------------
                 Sum[j in I(i)] W(i)

The sum runs over all j in the index set

    I(i) := { k : T(k) > T(i) }

R(i) = A(i) if I(i) empty, or the sum of weights is zero.
##namelist
namelist: The word namelist is used to denote a comma-separated list of
variable names. Namelists can be defined with the nlist command. In general,
if a command accepts a variable number of variable names, it also accepts
a namelist, or a list consisting both of variable names and namelists.
##ndvar               
ndvar: The ndvar command can be used to create dummy variables and 
interaction effects. The syntax is:

    ndvar(
        Y = dum(X),             create dummy variables    
        L = dmul(A,B),          create interaction effects   
        ...,                    optionally repeated       
    );       

##network analysis
network analysis: The following commands are available.

Command                                                     Section
-------------------------------------------------------------------
  gbcf      direct/indirect backward control                7.6.1.1
  gfc       measures of flow control                        7.6.1.3
  gfcf      direct/indirect forward control                 7.6.1.1
  gio       integrated ownership                            7.6.1.2

See also: graph data
##niset
niset: The command

    niset(
        rerr=,    relative error, def. rerr=1.e-4    
        aerr=,    absolute error, def. aerr=1.e-4             
    ) = n;        method (1 -- 5), def. 1         

selects an algorithm for numerical integration.
Available algorithms are:

    1. QNG    Piessens et al. [1983].
    2. QSUB   Patterson [1973].
    3. SNIFF  Garribba et al. [1978].
    4. QTRAP  Press et al. [1988, p. 121].
    5. QSIMP  Press et al. [1988, p. 123].

See also: int
##nlist                                                 
nlist: The nlist command can be used to define namelists. The syntax is:

    nlist(
        LName = V1,V2,...
    );  

where LName is the name of the namelist to be defined. The syntax for
names of namelists is the same as for variable names. The maximum number
of namelists is 50. The data1 command shows the currently defined
namelists. The clearnl command can be used to delete namelists.
##nncl
nncl: Nearest-neighbor clustering.

    nncl(
        alg=...,    algorithm, def. 1   
                    1 = nearest neighbors 
                    2 = mutual neighborhood
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1    
        sc=...,     threshold (alg=1), def. 1 
        ns=...,     size of neighborhood (alg=2), def. 2
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4
    ) = fname;           
    
All parameters, except for the name of an output file on the right-hand
side, are optional. The current relational data structure must specify
an undirected valued graph.
##noc
noc: NOC is a global variable that records the number of cases (rows)
in the data matrix that are currently selected. The value of NOC can be
found with the type 1 operators noc. For example,

    mpr(noc);

shows the current value of NOC.
        
See also: nocdm       
##nocdm
noc: NOCDM is a global variable that records the number of cases (rows)
in the data matrix before any case selection (see tsel) is applied. The
value of NOCDM can be found with the type 1 operators noc. For example,

    mpr(nocdm);

shows the current value of NOCDM.
        
See also: noc         
##npreg
npreg: The npreg command performs bivariate nonparametric regression.
The syntax is:

    npreg(                                      
        opt=...,    1 smoothed means            
                    2 smoothed quantiles        
                    3 smoothes frequencies      
                    4 lowess                    
                    5 midmeans                  
        k=...,      kernel, def. 1              
                    1 : uniform                 
                    2 : triangle                
                    3 : quartic                 
                    4 : Epanechnikov            
        d=...,      band width, def. 1.0
        x=...,      list of points for X axis   
        df=...,     output file                 
        fmt=...,    print format, def. 10.4     
        dtda=...,   TDA description
        sel=...,    case selection              
    ) = Y,X;                                    
                                                    
##nvar   
nvar: The nvar command creates a new data matrix, or adds variables to
an already existing data matrix. The syntax is:

    nvar( 
       vdef,          definition of a variable; this parameter can be
                      used several times to define several variables
                      simultaneously 
       noc=...,       maximum number of cases, def. 1000 
       isc=...,       separation character between entries 
       dfile=...,     definition of an external data file 
       dreclen=...,   fixed length of data file records 
       nmrec=...,     number of multiple records, def. 1 
       ffmt=...,      format information for data file records          
       match=...,     information about matching variables 

       isel=...,      case selection while reading data 
       vsel=...,      case selection while creating variables          
                      (cannot be used in block mode)
       break=...,     break on condition
                      (cannot be used in block mode)
       dblock=...,    definition of block mode 
       bsize=...,     maximum block size, def. 1000 
       bsel=...,      block mode case selection 

       fmt=...,       new print format 
       arcdic,        shows value labels for archive variables 

       mblnk=...,     new missing value code: blanks, def. -1 
       mstar=...,     new missing value code: stars, def. -1 
       mpnt=...,      new missing value code: points, def. -1 
       mmatch=...,    new missing value code: mismatches, def. -3 
       mgen=...,      new missing value code: general, no default 

       df=...,        creates an output data file 
       df1=...,       creates an output data file, only fist record of block
       keep=...,      keep variables for df option 
       drop=...,      drop variables for df option 
       sepc=...,      separation character for df/df1 option, def. blank
       l0=...,        leading blanks or zeros for df/df1 option, def. 0
                       0 : use leading blanks
                       1 : use leading zeros

       dtda=...,      TDA description file 
       dspss=...,     SPSS description file 
    );     

See also: multiple variables.
##optimal matching
optimal matching: See: seqm

##operators
operators: The following list shows all operators that can be used to
build expressions and functions. The AD column shows whether the 
operators can be used for automatic differentiation (0 no, 1 only first
derivatives, 2 first and second derivatives).

