ordinal probit: See: qreg

##pcyc
The pcyc command that can be used to calculate cycles in permutations.
The syntax is:

    pcyc(
        df=...,           output file (required)
        nfmt=...,         integer print format, def. 4
    ) = varlist;          list of variables (required)

On the right-hand side must be given a list of integer-valued variables,
say S1,...,Sn. It is assumed that these variables specify, for each record
in TDA's data matrix, a permutation of the integers 1,...,n, say

    s1,...,sn 

Permutations might be incomplete, that is, position i is considered as
as missing if si < 1 or si > n. However, cases where the same valid number
between 1 and n occurs two or more times are considered invalid and are 
ignored. For each valid case (permutation), the command calculates complete
and, if present, also incomplete cycles. The output file contains one
record for each valid case. The entries are as follows:

    RN          record number (case ID)
    NV          number of valid entries in the permutation
    NCC         number of complete cycles
    NIC         number of incomplete cycles
    LCC         total length of complete cycles
    LIC         total length of incomplete cycles
    S1,...,Sn   values of the input data (the permutation)

Finally follows a description of the cycles. Complete cycles are marked by
round brackets, incomplete cycles are marked by square brackets.
##pdata
pdata: The pdata command writes variables from the current data matrix
to an external data file. The syntax is:

    pdata(      
        keep=...,      keep specified variables   
        drop=...,      drop specified variables   
        sort=...,      sort according to specified variables   
        noc=...,       write only first ... cases   
        nn = n1,n2,    use only rows n1 -- n2
        transp,        transpose data matrix 
        nc = n,        create a new record after each n variables   
        nq = n,        concatenate n records, def. 1   
        ap = 1,        append data to output file, def. ap = 0     
        prn =...,      option for writing single variables, def. prn = 0
                        1 : write single variable as triangle matrix  
                        2 : write single variable as square matrix  
                        3 : write data matrix as a single column vector
        l0 = ...,      control printing of numerical values, def. 0
                        0 : leading blanks remain empty
                        1 : fill leading blanks with zeros
        sepc=...,      separation character, def. blank
                        sepc = none,  no separation character
                        sepc = c,     use c as separation character
        dtda=...,      create a TDA description file   
        dspss=...,     create an SPSS description file             
        sd=...,        sd = 1 writes coordinates of a spatial data file,
                       (this requires a spatial data matrix), def. 0
        sdfmt=...,     print format for coordinates, def. 12.4

    ) = fname;          

##pdatd
pdatd: The pdatd command can be used to create distance matrices. The
syntax is:

    pdatd (                                                 
        v=...,      optional varlist, def. all variables    
        opt=...,    option, def. 1                          
                      1 euclidean distances                   
                      2 city-block distance                               
                      3 number of different values                        
                      4 dissimilarity index     /

        wt=...,     optional weights, def. none                       
        fmt = ...,  print format, def. 10.4                 
    ) = fname;      output file                             

Based on the selected variables and depending on opt, the command
creates a distance matrix with NOC rows and columns and writes the
matrix into the output file specified at the right-hand side.

##pdatr
pdatr: The pdatr command writes data for all pairs of cases i and j
(i,j = 1,...,NOC) to the output file specified by fname.
      
    pdatr(
        keep = varlist,
        drop = varlist,
        nfmt = print format for i,j, def. 4
    ) = fname;

##plabel
plabel: The command

    plabel(
        sc=...,     distance from top of coordinate system, def. 0 (mm)
        fs=...,     font size, def. 3 (mm)
    ) = string;        

can be used to place a label at the top of a coordinate system.
##plcurv3
plcurv3. The command

    plcurv3(                                                              
        rx=...,     range of argument: a(d)b                              
        f1=...,     x1 = f(x)                                             
        f2=...,     x2 = f(x)                                             
        f3=...,     x3 = f(x)                                             
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line)                        
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm)                             
        nc=...,     1 if no clipping                                      
    );                                                                    
         
can be used to plot a parametrically defined curve in a 3d coordinate system 
defined with the psetup3 command. Example:

    psfile = xx.ps;
    psetup3(
        pxa = -1.1,1.1,
        pya = -1.1,1.1,
        pza = -1.1,1.1,
    );
    plcurv3(
        nc=1,
        rx = -1(0.1)20,
        f1 = cos(x),
        f2 = sin(x),
        f3 = 0.1 * x,
    );

##ple
ple: The ple command can be used for product-limit (Kaplan-Meier)
estimation. The syntax is:

    ple(     
        grp=,       group indicator variables, def. without groups 
        csf,        compare survivor functions
        qo=,        request table with quantiles, no default
        qt=,        request table with quantiles, no default
        fmt=,       print format for output file, def. fmt=7.5
        ap=1,       append data to end of output file
        prot=,      protocol for test statistic, no default
        pfmt=,      print format for protocol file, def. pfmt=-19.11
    ) = name_of_output_file;     

The command requires a definition of episode data with the edef command.

See also: ltb, grp, prot, mple
##plframe
plframe: The command

    plframe(
        lt=...,       line type, def. 1 (solid line)   
        lw=...,       line width, def. 0.2 (mm)   
        gs=...,       grey scale value, def. 1 (white)
    );

can be used to plot a frame around the coordinate system.
##plg
plg: The plg command can be used to plot a graph. It required a valid
PostScript output file, defined with the psfile and psetup commands.

    plg(      
        nmax=...,        maximal number of nodes, def. 100 
        node(            place node at (x,y)  
            n=...,       external node number, must be integer
            gt=...,      type of display, def. 1 
                         1 = circle       
                         2 = square       
                         3 = rectangle plus semi-circles
                         4 = simple rectangle  
            str=...,     optional text if not node number
            gt=...,      type of display, def. 1 
            rd=...,      size of display in mm 
            lt=...,      line type, def. 1 
            lw=...,      line width, def. 0.2 mm 
            gs=...,      gray scale value, def. 1 (white)
            fs=...,      font size 
        ) = x,y,              

        edge(            connect nodes i and j
            ic=...,      optional edge value 
            fs=...,      font size for edge value
            lt=...,      line type, def. 1 
            lw=...,      line width, def. 0.2 mm   
            a=...,       size of optional arrow head   
            rd=...,      radius for arcs, in mm            
            dir=...,     location of loop           
        ) = i,j,           
    );      

For further information see Section 3.6.5 of the Manual.
##plot
plot: The command

    plot(
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        s=...,      number of marker symbol 
        fs=...,     size of marker symbol, def. 2 (mm) 
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white) 
        r=...,      creates step function  
        dir=...,    type of step function  
        ns=...,     if ns=1 plot only horizontal lines
        a=...,      creates an arrow 
        nc=...,     no clipping option, def. 0           
        sel=...,    selection of data points
    ) = VX,VY;

plots the points given by variables VX and VY into the current
PostScript file. By default, the points are connected by lines.

See also: plotp
##plot3
plot3. The command

    plot3(                                                    
        geo=...,        1 use geographic coordinates, def. 0  
        lt=...,         line type, def. 1 (solid line)        
        lw=...,         line width, def. 0.2 (mm)              
        gs=...,         grey scale value, def. 1 (white)      
        s=...,          number of marker symbol                         
        fs=...,         size of marker symbol, def. 2 (mm)                  
        a=...,          creates an arrow                          
        nc=...,         no clipping option, def. 0                                
    ) = VX,VY,VZ;       points                                
                                                                        
plots the points given by the variables on the right hand side in a
3-dimensional coordinate system (defined the the psetup3 command).
Most parameters have the same meaning a in the 2-dimensional plot command.
In addition, the geo parameter can be used to select between two types of
coordinates:

If geo = 0 (default), the values of VX, VY and VZ are interpreted as standard
cartesian coordinates in the given coordinate system.

If geo = 1, the values are interpreted as geographical coordinates: VX
provides the longitude (lon), VY provides the latitude (lat), and VZ 
provides the distance from the origin (r). It is required that
-180 <= lon, lat <= +180 and r >= 0.

See also: plotp3
##plotc  
plotc: The plotc command can be used for contour plots. Syntax is:

    plotc(  
        x=...,     specification of levels 
        nn=...,    grid specification, def. nn=10,10    
        lt=...,    line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,    line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        gs=...,    grey scale value, def. 1 (white) 
    ) = function;             

The right-hand side must contain the definition of a function depending
(up to) two parameters, specified by the key words x1 (for the x axis)
and x2 (for the y axis). 
##plotch 
plotch: The command 

    plotch(     
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        s=...,      marker symbol 
        fs=...,     size of marker symbol, def. 2 (mm) 
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white) 
        nc=...,     no clipping option, def. 0           
        ns=...,     number of intervals for smoothing           
        ic=...,     added to smoothed hull, def. 0           
        sel=...,    selection of data points
    ) = VX,VY;

plots a convex hull around the points given by the variables on the
right-hand side.
