##plotd  
plotd: The plotd command can be used to plot a variable's density,
based on a method of kernel density estimation. The syntax is:

    plotd(                                                                
        x=...,      sequence of evaluation points, required               
        d=...,      bandwidth, def. 1.0                                   
        k=...,      kernel, def. 1                                        
                    1 : uniform                                           
                    2 : triangle                                          
                    3 : quartic                                           
                    4 : Epanechnikov                                      
        dscal=...,  scaling factor, def. 1.0
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line)                        
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm)                             
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white)                      
        nc=...,     nc=1 for no clipping, def. 0                            
        df=...,     print estimates to output file                            
        fmt=...,    print format for output file, def. 10.4               
    ) = variable;                                                         

    There must be exactly one variable name on the right-hand side. Also
    required is the x parameter to specify a sequence of evaluation points
    on the x axis, the syntax is

        x = x1,...,xn   or   x=x1(d)xn

    As an option, the estimated density data are written to an output
    file specified with the df option.

##plote  
plote: The plote command can be used to plot ellipses.

    plote(  
        xy=...,     coordinates of center 
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white) 
        nc=...,     no clipping option, def. 0 
    ) = a,b [,r];}            

The xy=x,y parameter must be used to define the center of the ellipse;
a is the half length of the main axis (interpreted in user coordinates of
the x axis), b is the half length of the second axis of the ellipse
(interpreted in user coordinates of the y axis). r is optional, if 
provided the ellipse is rotated by r degrees.
##plotf  
plotf: The plotf command can be used to plot a function. Syntax is:

    plotf(     
        rx=...,     range of function argument       
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line)   
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm)   
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white)   
        nc=...,     no clipping option, def. 0 
    ) = function;               

The right-hand side must provide the definition of a function, depending
on a single argument. The function is evaluated in the range [a,b] on
the x axis, with increments of size d; these values must be specified
with the parameter:

    rx = a (d) b,

See also: xplotf

##ploth  
ploth: The ploth command can be used to plot a histogram. Syntax is:

    ploth(                                                            
        x=...,      intervals, required                               
        w=...,      variable for weights                              
        dscal=...,  scaling factor, def. 1.0
        s=...,      s=0 (default) classes are left closed             
                    s=1 classes are left open                         
        ns=...,     ns=1, don't plot vertical lines, def. ns=0        
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line)                    
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm)                         
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white)                  
        nc=...,     nc=1 if no clipping, def. 0                        
        df=...,     print table to output file                        
        fmt=...,    print format for output file, def. 10.4           
    ) = variable;                                                     

    There must be exactly one variable name on the right-hand side. Also
    one has to use the x parameter to specify a set of intervals (classes)
    for the histogram. The syntax is

        x=x1,x2,...,xn,   or   x=x1(d)x2,

    x1 must be not greater than the minimum of the values of the variable,
    and xn must not smaller than the maximum of the values of the variable.
    The command requires a valid setup for plots and uses the currently
    defined coordinate system. The heights of the histogram are calculated
    in such a way that the areas equal the frequencies. A corresponding 
    frequency table can be requested with the df parameter. The output file
    will then contain n - 1 lines with the following entries:

    1) begin of class
    2) end of class
    3) absolute frequency
    4) relative frequency 
    5) height of histogram

See also: atab   
##plotk  
plotk: The plotk command can be used to plot arcs, optionally ending
in an arrow. The syntax is:

    plotk(  
        sc=...,     degree of curvature, def. 0 
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        a=...,      size of arrow head 
        nc=...,     no clipping option, def. 0 
    ) = x1,y1,...,xn,yn;             

##plotm
plotm: The command

    plotm (parameter) = X1,Y1,X2,Y2,...;

where X1,Y1,... are data matrix variables, plots a polygon

    (X1,Y1), (X2,Y2), ...,
                          
separately for each case in the data matrix. Parameters are optional and
identical with those for the the plot command.
##ploto  
ploto: The ploto command can be used to plot circles and arcs.       

    ploto(      
        xy=...,     coordinates of center 
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white) 
        nc=...,     no clipping option, def. 0 
    ) = r [,alpha,beta];}            

The xy=x,y parameter must be used to define the center of the circle.
The radius must be given on the right-hand  side. The angles, alpha and
beta, to be given in degrees, are optional. If these angles are not given,
the result is a full circle, otherwise it is an arc, beginning at the
angle alpha and ending at the angle beta, in counterclockwise direction.
##plotp
plotp: The command

    plotp(  
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        s=...,      marker symbol 
        fs=...,     size of marker symbol, def. 2 (mm) 
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white) 
        r=...,      creates step function  
        dir=...,    type of step function  
        ns=..,      if ns=1 plot only horizontal lines
        a=...,      creates an arrow 
        nc=...,     no clipping option, def. 0
    ) = x1,y1,...,xn,yn;             

plots the points given on the right-hand side into the current
PostScript file. By default, the points are connected by lines.

See also: plot
##plotp3
plotp3. The command

    plotp3(                                                    
        geo=...,        1 use geographic coordinates, def. 0  
        lt=...,         line type, def. 1 (solid line)        
        lw=...,         line width, def. 0.2 (mm)              
        gs=...,         grey scale value, def. 1 (white)      
        s=...,          number of marker symbol                         
        fs=...,         size of marker symbol, def. 2 (mm)                  
        a=...,          creates an arrow                          
        nc=...,         no clipping option, def. 0                                
    ) = x1,y1,z1,x2,y2,z2,...; points                         
                                                                        
plots the points given on the right hand side in a 3-dimensional coordinate
system (defined the the psetup3 command). All parameters have the same
meaning as explained for the plot3 command.

See also: plot3 
##plots  
plots: The command 

    plots(     
        ns=...,     degree of smoothing, def. 2  
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        s=...,      marker symbol 
        fs=...,     size of marker symbol, def. 2 (mm) 
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white) 
        nc=...,     no clipping option, def. 0           
        sc=...,     request closed curve (sc=1), def. sc=0            
        df=...,     write data to output file           
        fmt=...,    print format           
        sel=...,    selection of data points 
    ) = VX,VY;               

can be used for smoothing polygons given by the variables on the
right-hand side.
##plrec  
plrec: The command

    plrec(
        lt=...,      line type, def. 1 (solid line) 
        lw=...,      line width, def. 0.2 (mm) 
        gs=...,      grey scale value, def. 1 (white) 
        r=...,       rotation, def. 0  
    ) = x,y,xlen,ylen;             

plots a rectangle with lower left corner at point (x,y); xlen is
the width and ylen is the heigth of the rectangle.
##plsurf3
plsurf3. The command

    plsurf3(                                                              
        ru=...,     range of first argument: ua,ub,nu,mu                  
        rv=...,     range of second argument: va,vb,nv,mv                 
        f1=...,     x1 = f(u,v)                                           
        f2=...,     x2 = f(u,v)                                           
        f3=...,     x3 = f(u,v)                                           
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1 (solid line)                        
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 (mm)                             
        cont=...,   cont=1 adds internal contour lines, def. 0                        
        gs=...,     grey scale value, def. 1 (white)
        nc=...,     1 if no clipping                                      
    );                                                                    

can be used to plot a parametrically defined surface. The surface is evaluated
on a grid [ua <= u <= ub] and [va <= v <= vb]; nu and nv are the number of
points on the grid in the u and v direction, respectively, where the 
function is evaluated. In addition, mu and mv specify the number of grid   
lines that are used to plot the surface. Example:

    psfile = xx.ps;                    
    psetup3(
        pxa   = -3,3,              
        pya   = -3,3,             
        pza   = -3,3,
        view = 40, 20,   
    );
    plsurf3 (
        rv = -3,3,30,10,            
        ru = -3,3,30,10,  
        f1 = u,                                
        f2 = v,                              
        f3 = sin(u * u + v * v) / (u * u + v * v + 0.00001),
        cont = 1,
    );

##pltext
pltext: The command

    pltext(
        xy=...,     coordinates 
        fs=...,     font size, def. 2 (mm) 
        sc=...,     sc=1 centers the string, def. 0  
        r=...,      rotation, def. 0       
        s=...,      number of a marker symbol
        wf=...,     if wf=1 the string is drawn in a white
                    bounding box, def. 0
    ) = string;             

plots string at position xy.
##pltext3
pltext3. The command

    pltext3(                                                      
        xyz=x,y,z,  coordinates                                   
        fs=...,     font size, def. 2 (mm)                      
        sc=...,     sc = 1 centers the string, def. 0                      
        r=...,      rotation, def. 0                          
        s=...,      number of a marker symbol                             
        wf=...,     if wf=1 the string is drawn in a white
                    bounding box, def. 0
    ) = string;                                                    

plots string at the position (x,y,z).
##pltree
pltree: The pltree command can be used to plot a tree. The command requires
a valid PostScript output file defined with psfile and psetup. 

    pltree(     
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1          
        gt=...,     type of graph (1 or 2), def. 2    
        rt=...,     number of root node             
        pl=...,     type of plot (1,2,3), def. 3   
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1            
        lw=...,     line width in mm, def. 0.2            
        fs=...,     font size in mm, def. 2            
        nc=...,     label until node ...            
        ni=1,       label only for leaves, def. 0            
    );     

Type of display is: pl=1 : radial drawing,
                    pl=2 : hierarchical drawing,
                    pl=3 : axial drawing.

See also: ptree
##plxa 
plxa: The plxa and plya commands can be used to plot, respectively,    
x and y axes of a coordinate system. The commands require a valid
PostScript plot environment. Syntax is:

    plxa(
        sc=...,       distance of main tick marks   
        ic=...,       number of sub-intervals   
        lt=...,       line type, def. 1 (solid line)   
        lw=...,       line width, def. 0.2 (mm)   
        fs=...,       font size for labels, def. 2 (mm)   
        fmt=...,      print format for labels, def. 0.0   
        tl=...,       length of tick marks, def. 1.8 (mm)   
        dir=...,      direction of axis, def. 0
        off=...,      value added to numerical labels, def. 0.0
    ) = xa,ya,xb,yb;               

See also: psfile, psetup
##plxgrid
plxgrid: The plxgrid and plygrid commands can be used to place grid
lines into a coordinate system. The syntax is:

    plxgrid
        lt=...,       line type, def. 1 (solid line)   
        lw=...,       line width, def. 0.05 (mm)   
    ) = y1,y2,...;
           
and analoguously for plygrid.
##plya 
plya: See plxa
##poisson regression
poisson regression: There is no special command for Poisson regression.
However, one can use the fml command, see the discussion in Section
6.14.1 in the Manual.

##principal components
principal components. See: dma

##print
print: The command

    print(string);

writes string into the standard output. In order to preserve blank
characters, string should be enclosed in single or double quotation marks.
##probit
probit: See: qreg
##procrustes rotation
procrustes rotation: See mproc
##prot
prot: The parameter

    prot = name_of_protocol_file,    

can be used in connection with commands that use one of TDA's 
algorithms for function minimization. It creates a protocol file that
contains information about the iterations of the algorithm.

More information about parameter values and derivatives (if any) can be
requested by using prot1 instead of prot. (An additional option, prot2, is
only for test purposes. 

The print format for the protocol file can be controlled with the 
                    
    pfmt = print_format,                       

parameter. The default is pfmt=-19.11. If the prot parameter specifies
the name of an already existing file, it is overwritten without warning.
One can use prota, instead of prot, to request that the new information
is appended to the the end of an already existing file. 
##psclose
psclose: The command

    psclose;         

without arguments closes a currently open PostScript file.

See also: psfile, xlog 
##psetup
psetup: The psetup command prepares a PostScript file that has been
opened with the psfile command for accepting plot objects. The syntax is:

    psetup(
        pxa=...,     definition of x axis   
        pya=...,     definition of y axis   
        pxlen=...,   length of x axis in mm, def. pxlen=120     
        pylen=...,   length of y axis in mm, def. pylen=80    
        psorg=...,   PostScript coordinates of origin, def. psorg=150,460
        psscal=...,  scaling factors, def. psscal=1,1     
        psrot=...,   rotation, def. 0     
    );               

See also: psfile 
##psetupg
psetupg. The psetupg command is used to set up a plot environment for 
the drawing of geographical data. 

    psetupg(                                                              
        proj=...,   typ of projection, def. 10                            
                    10  cylindrical (equidistant)                         
                    11  cylindrical (equal-area)                          
                    12  cylindrical (Mercator)                            
                    20  azimuthal (orthographic)                          
        view=...,   direction: lon,lat, default 0,0                       
        region=..., dlon,dlat (no default)                                
        rhem=...,   size of hemisphere, def. 90                           
        pxlen=...,  width of plot in mm, def. 120                         
        pylen=...,  height of plot in mm, def. depends on proj            
        psorg=...,  origin of Postscript coordinate system, def. 100,100  
        psrot=...,  rotation, def. 0     
    );                                                                    

See also: sdpgrat, sdpgeo, sdpmap.
##psetup3
psetup3. The psetup3 command prepares a PostScript file that has been
opened with the psfile command for accepting 3-dimensionale plot objects.

    psetup3(                                                    
        view=lon,lat,     direction of projection (degrees), def. 30,30   
        pxa=xa,xb,        logical x axis from xa to xb          
        pya=ya,yb,        logical y axis from ya to yb          
        pza=za,zb,        logical z axis from za to zb          
        pxlen=...,        horizontal size of plot (mm), def 100 
        psorg=...,        origin of PostScript coord. system, def. 100,100 
        psscal=...,       scaling, def. 1,1                     
        psrot=...,        rotation, def. 0                      
    );                                                          

This command defines a 3d user coordinate system and, with the view  
parameter, a direction for parallel projection of all further plot objects.
Values are restricted to the following ranges:

    -180 <= lon <= +180, -90 <= lat <= +90

Note also that only the horizontal size of the plot can be specified.    
##psfile       
psfile: The command

    psfile = fname;

opens a new PostScript file, fname. The file remains open until explictly
closed with the psclose command, another psfile command is given, or a new
PostScript file is created with xopen or xplot commands.

See also: psclose, psetup, xopen, xlog, xplot
##ptree
ptree: The ptree command can be used to print a tree data structure
into an output file. The syntax is:

    ptree(     
        gn=...,     graph number, def. 1        
        rt=...,     root of the tree             
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4          
        fmt=...,    print format for values, def. 10.4
    ) = fname;           

See also: pltree
##qreg
qreg: The qreg estimates quantal response models, in particular, logit
and probit models. The syntax is:

    qreg(
        m=...,        model selection, def. 1 
                       1 = binary logit      
                       2 = binary probit     
                       3 = ordinal logit      
                       4 = ordinal probit     
                       5 = multinomial logit 
                       6 = multivariate probit 
                       7 = conditional logit 
                       8 = simultaneous binary probits 
        ni=1,         if without intercept, def. 0  
        nq=...,       number of categories if m=5    
        nw=...,       number of waves, def. 1  
        pmin=...,     minimum number of valid wave data, def. 1 
        tfmt=...,     print format for results, def. 10.4  
        ppar=...,     print estimated coefficients to output file 
        pcov=...,     print covariance matrix to output file 
        res=...,      additional output options 
        mfmt=...,     print format for pcov option, def. 12.4      
        df=...,       print data to an output file 
        dtda=...,     TDA description of output file 
        fmt=...,      print format for df option, def. 10.4       
        opt=...,      model-specific options, def. 1  
        nhp=...,      control of numerical integration, def. 6  
        eps=...,      accuracy of numerical integration, def. 10e-6
        mplog=...,    write loglikelihood value into matrix
        mppar=...,    write parameters into matrix
        mpcov=...,    write covariance matrix into matrix
        mpgrad=...,   write gradients into matrix
    ) = varlist;

varlist is a comma-separated list of variables. The first variable in
varlist is interpreted as the dependent variable.

See also: mpcov, mpgrad, mplog, mppar
##quant
quant: The quant command can be used to calculate quantiles for a set
of variables. The syntax is:

    quant(     
        fmt=...,     print format, def. 7.2   
        df=...,      output file            
        mppar=...,   matrix name
    ) = varlist;    

The command calculates quantiles corresponding to the orders

    0.1  0.2  0.25  0.3  0.4  0.5  0.6  0.7  0.75  0.8  0.9       

See also: mppar
##random numbers
The following type 1 operators are available for random number generation.

    rd          provides random numbers equally distributed in (0,1).

    rd(a,b)     provides random numbers equally distributed in (a,b).

    grd(V)      provides random numbers equally distributed in (0,1);
                a new random number is only generated in the first row of
                the data matrix, or if the variable V changes its value.

    rdn, rdn1   provides standard normally distributed random numbers.

    rdp1(z)     creates random numbers distributed according to a
                Poisson distribution with mean z. z must be a postive
                number in the interval (0,350).

    rdmn(i,S)   creates random numbers that follow a multivariate normal
                distribution with correlation matrix S. The operators
                requires that a complete symmetric and positive definite
                matrix, S, is defined with the mdef command. rdmn(i,S)
                returns the i.th component of the random vector.

##range
range: This is an experimental command to find the range of a function 
with the help of interval calculations. The syntax is:

    range(                                                  
        ns=...,         controls usage of derivatives, def. 0
                        0  command will not use derivatives               
                        1  command will use derivatives     
        xp=...,         starting values plus boxes          
        dsv=...,        starting values provided by a file  
        mxit=...,       max number of iterations, def. 100  
        nbox=...,       max number of boxes, def. 100       
        tolbw=...,      tolerance box width, def. 1.e-6     
        tolfd=...,      tolerance for function range, 1e-10 
        tolfe=...,      for global min/max, def. 1.e-10     
        fmt=...,        print format, def. 13.6             
        prot=...,       protocol file                       
    ) = function;                                           

##rate
rate: The rate command estimates transition rate models. The syntax is:

    rate(     
        tp=...,         time periods 
        xa(j,k)=...,    variables for A-term of model 
        xb(j,k)=...,    variables for B-term of model 
        xc(j,k)=...,    variables for C-term of model 
        xd(j,k)=...,    variables for D-term of model 
        deg=...,        degree of polynomial rate, def. 0 
        kgam=...,       coefficient for gamma model, def. 1 
        mix=...,        selection of mixture model
        grp=...,        groups for stratified Cox model 
        rrisk,          print table with relative risks 
        ppar=...,       output file with estimated parameters 
        pcov=...,       output file with covariance matrix 
        pres=...,       generalized residuals
        prate=...,      calculation of estimated rates                  
        tfmt=...,       print format for results, def. 10.4  
        mfmt=...,       print format for pcov, pres and prate, def. 12.4
        dsv=...,        input file with starting values 
        con=...,        linear parameter constraints 
                        in addition, one can use all parameters to 
                        control maximum likelihood estimation.
        mplog=...,      write loglikelihood value into matrix
        mppar=...,      write parameters into matrix
        mpcov=...,      write covariance matrix into matrix
        mpgrad=...,     write gradients into matrix
    ) = model_number;     

For available models, see: rate model numbers

See also: frml, mpcov, mpgrad, mplog, mppar
##rate model numbers
rate model numbers: The following models can be selected with the
rate command:

    1    Cox model 
    2    Exponential model 
    3    Piecewise constant exponential model 
    4    Polynomial rates, I 
    5    Polynomial rates, II 
    6    Gompertz-Makeham models 
    7    Weibull model  
    8    Sickle  model  
    9    Log-logistic model, type I 
   10    Log-logistic model, type II 
   12    Log-normal model 
   13    Generalized gamma model 
   14    Inverse Gaussian model 
   16    Piecewise constant exponential model 
         with period-specific effects 
   20    Logistic regression  model   
   21    Complementary log-log model 

See: rate
##rcorr
rcorr: The rcorr command can be used to calculate the rank correlation
matrix (Kendall's tau) for a set of variables. The syntax is:

    rcorr(      
        fmt=...,      print format, def. 7.4    
        df=...,       output file          
        prn=...,      data format in output file, def. 0
        mpcov=...,    write result into matrix
    ) = varlist;

If varlist is not specified, the command uses all currently defined
variables.

There are five possible output formats:

    prn = 0   lower triangle and main diagonal          
          1   full square matrix          
          2   column vector: lower triangle          
          3   column vector: lower triangle and main diagonal          
          4   column vector: full matrix          

Note that this command ignores case weights.
See also: cov, corr  

##rdbf
rdbf: The command

    rdbf(                                 
       df=...,      output file              
    ) = fname;      dBase input file.       

tries to read the dBase file given on the right-hand side. If an output
file is specified with the optional df parameter, the contents of the
dBase file (only numerical and character fields) will be written to
this file. 

##recode
recode: The recode command can be used to change the values of already 
existing variables. The syntax is:

    recode(
        dblock=...,              definition of block mode      
        VName[fmt]=expression,   recode variable VName     
        ...                      optionally repeated       
    );

##regression
regression: See the commands

    lsreg       least squares regression
    l1reg       L1 norm regression
    qreg        quantal response (logit and probit) models
    freg        nonlinear regression

##repeat 
repeat: The repeat/endrepeat pair of commands can be used to request
repetition of commands. The command can be used in two ways:

a)  repeat (n=expr);
    ...
    ...
    endrepeat;

    where expr is a scalar expression that evaluates to a positive integer,
    say m. The commands in between repeat and endrepeat are then repeated
    m times.

b)  repeat (n=expr, I );
    ...
    ...
    endrepeat;

    Given in this form, the command creates a 1 x 1 matrix,    
    I (arbitrary matrix name), and sets I(1,1) to the value of the
    iteration counter for the repeat loop. It is required that
    a matrix with the given name (I) does not already exists.

See also: break, while, if      
##repsel
repsel:          
        repsel(
            id = V,         # optional ID variable
        )   = S;            # matrix expression

S is a matrix-expression with dimension BNOC x 1. The command requires that
BNOC has been set with the dblock command. Like the dblock command, the 
repsel command turns off any possibly active tsel (temporary case) selection.
The repsel command works in two different ways, depending on whether the
optional id parameter is used.

a)  If the id parameter is not used, the command creates a new
    data matrix where the cases in block i (i=1,...,BNOC) are
    repeated S(i,1) times, or omitted if S(i,1) <= 0.

b)  If the name of a variable, say V, is given with the id parameter,
    the command creates a new data matrix where block i consists of
    those cases where the value of V equals S(i,1).

The repsel case selection remains valid until

-   a new repsel command is given,
-   the data matrix is cleared,
-   a tsel command is given,
-   a dblock command is given, or
-   the command is explicitly turned off with

    repsel = off;

See: dblock
##rspss
rspss: The rspss command can be used to read an SPSS portable file.
The syntax is:

    rspss(     
        len=...,           record length    
        noc=...,           maximum number of cases    
        msys=...,          system missing value code, def. -5     
        fmt=...,           new print format               
        dvar(fn=...)=...,  create variable description file           
        df=...,            write data to output file                        
        arcd=...,          write archive description file
    ) = fname;             

If the SPSS file contains string variables these variables are created as part
of TDA's internal data matrix or, when using the df option, they are directly
written to the output file.

The df, dvar, and arcd parameter can be used in the same way as described for
the rstata command.

See also: wspss, rspss1
##rspss1
rspss1: The command

    rspss1(                                                   
        noc=...,            # of records to read, def. all    
        dvar=...,           file containing value label information
        msys=...,           system missing value code, def. -5  
        df=...,             write data to output file             
    ) = file_name;                                            

can be used to read SPSS sav-files (created by SPSS for Windows on an Intel
platform, reversed byte order). By default, the data are read into a TDA
data matrix (this requires that a data matrix is not already present).
Alternatively one can use the df parameter in order to write the data
directly to an output file.

See also: wspss1, rspss
##rstata
rstata: The rstata command can be used to read a Stata file, supported are
Stata releases 4, 6, 7 and 10. The syntax is:

    rstata(                                                 
        noc=...,         # of records to read, def. all      
        msys=...,        system missing value code, def. -5    
        df=...,          write data directly to output file  
        dvar(...)=...,   create/update variable description file         
        arcd=...,        create/update archive description file            
        n=...,           treatment of variable names
                         1 : default
                         2 : translate to upper case letters
    ) = fname;

If the Stata file contains string variables these variables are created as
part of TDA's internal data matrix or, when using the df option, they are directly
written to the output file. 

Note: Reading of value labels is only supportes for Stata releases 6 and 7.

a) If the df parameter is used as  

    dfa = file_name,

data are appended to the specified file; otherwise, with df=..., a new file is
created.

b) The dvar parameter has the syntax

    dvar(argument1,argument2,...) = file_name,

The arguments are optional and can be:

    fn=file_number,     this file number is used in the
                        variable description file, default is 1.

    p=n,                where n is an integer. Each string variable
                        will then be partitioned into parts of
                        n characters length, and corresponding
                        variable definitions are added to the
                        variable description file. The additional
                        variables are of type string.

    pn=n,               same as p=n, but the new variables are of
                        type numerical.

    p(VNAME)=n,         where VNAME is the name of a variable and
                        n is a positive integer. Then, if VNAME
                        is a string variable, only this variable
                        is partitioned into parts of length n. The
                        additional variables are of type string.

    pn(VNAME)=n,        same as p(VNAME)=n, but the new variables
                        are of type numerical.

Note that only one p=n or pn=n parameter can be used. However, the p(VNAME)=n
and pn(VNAME)=n parameters are compatible and can be used several times.

The dvar parameter can also be used as

    dvara(...)=...,

New variable descriptions are then appended to an already existing file.

c) The arcd parameter has syntax

    arcd(parameter1,parameter2) = file_name,

where the optional arguments can be:

    zoo = file_name,        this file name is then used in the
                            archive description file for the
                            ZOO archive.

    vdf = file_name,        this file name is then used in the
                            archive description file for the
                            variable description file. The file
                            number for this file is always 999.

Note that the arcd command is only recognized if the rstata command contains
both a df and a dvar parameter. The file name used with the vdf parameter should
be the same as that used in the dvar parameter (in order to get valid information
about number of records).

d) The additional options can be used to ease the creation of a TDA data archive.
For example, the following command file would create an archive:

    $"rm xxx.zoo";
    rstata(
        dvar(fn=1) = dv,
        df = df,
        arcd(zoo=xxx.zoo,vdf=dv) = arcd,
    ) = xxx.dta;
    $"zoo ah xxx df";
    $"zoo ah xxx dv";
    arcd = arcd;
    arcc;

e)  Note: when creating a variable description file TDA tries to recognize value
labels which, in a Stata file, are stored at the end of the file. Since this
requires that all data has been read, value labels are only recognized if the noc
parameter is not used.

See also: wstata
##rsys
rsys: The command

    rsys [= name of a TDA system file];

reads a TDA system file previously created with the wsys command. The   
default file name is "tda.sys".

See also: wsys
##scatterplot
scatterplot: See the commands

    scplot, xplot

##scplot
scplot: The scplot command can be used to create scatterplots. The
syntax is:

    scplot(      
        opt=...,     type of plot, def. 1    
                      1 = only symbols       
                      2 = sunflower plot     
                      3 = lowess regression  
        s=...,       symbol type for opt=1, def. 0 
        fs=...,      symbol size for opt=1, def. 2 (mm) 
        nn=...,      grid definition for opt=2, def. nn=1,1      
        fss=...,     symbol size for opt=2, def. 2 (mm)      
        lt=...,      line type for regression lines, def. 1    
        lw=...,      line width for regression lines, def. 0.2 (mm)   
        sig=...,     smoothing factor for opt=3, def. 0.5          
        d=...,       optional parameter for opt=3, def. 0            
        ns=...,      optional parameter for opt=3, def. 0          
        df=...,      output from lowess regression                 
        fmt=...,     print format for df option, def. 10.4         
        sel=...,     case select option                            
        nc=...,      no clipping if nc=1, def. 0             
    ) = X,Y;               

The command requires a valid PostScript plot environment defined with
psfile and psetup.

See also: xplot
##sddcwp
sddcwp: This command is intended to transform a Polygon Point ASCII file from
the DCW (Digital Chart of the World) project into a TDA spatial data file.
The command has the following syntax:
   
    sddcwp(                                                       
        df=...,     output file (required)                        
        nc=...,     if nc=1 do not include centroids, def. 0
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4                  
        fmt=...,    floating point print format, def. 12.6        
    ) = input_file;                                               
                                                                        
Input files can be obtained from www.maproom.psu.edu/dcw/. The file format
must conform to the option DCW Polygon Point Generation. See Section 9.4.3.1
in the Manual for an example.
##sddf
sddf: This command calculates the distribution function of a set-valued
discrete variable. The syntax is:

    sddf(                                               
        opt=...,        option, def. 1
                        1 calculate min, max, and mean distribution funktion
                        2 calculate also a self-consistent distr function
        mxit=...,       max number of iterations, def. 50         
        tolf=...,       tolerance, def. 1e-3            
        df=...,         optional output file            
        prot=...,       protocol file                   
    ) = X1,...,Xm;      components of set-valued variable
                                                        
See also: iddf, idf
##sde00
sde00: Conversion of Arc/Info export (E00) files into TDA spatial data files.
                                                                        
    sde00(                                                        
        df=...,         output file (optional)                    
        nc=...,         option for polygon coverages, def. 0      
                        0 = drop first (universal) polygon        
                        1 = keep this polygon                     
        attr=...,       option for attributes, def. 0             
                        0 = drop all attribute variables            
                        1 = drop only default variables           
                        2 = include all variables                 
        fmt=...,        floating point print format, def. 12.6    
        dtda=...,       name of TDA description file              
    ) = name_of_e00_file;                                         
                                                                        
This command reads an uncompressed E00 ASCII file. Without an output file,
the command reads the file and provides summary information about the
sections. Given the name of an output file with the df parameter, the command
tries to create a TDA spatial data file based on the contens of the E00 file.
There are three interpretations.

(1) If there is no ARC section but a LAB section, interpretation is based
    on the assumption of a point coverage. All spatial objects in the output
    file will be of type 1 (points).         

(2) If there is an ARC section but no PAL section, interpretation is based on
    the assumption of a line coverage. All spatial objects in the output file
    will be of type 2 (lines).                                          

(3) If there is an ARC section and a PAL section, interpretation is based on
    the assumption of a polygon coverage. All spatial objects in the output 
    file will be of type 3 (polygons). By default, the first polygon (the       
    so-called universal polygon) is not written into the output file. To  
    include this polygon one can use the nc=1 parameter.

The attr parameter can be used to request that attribute variables which
might be available in the input file are added to the output file. The
options are:

attr = 0    no attribute variables added (default)
attr = 1    except for the Arc/Info default variables, all other attribute
            variables are added to the output file                      
attr = 2    all variables are added to the output file

For point and polygon coverages, attribute variables are taken from the PAT
section, for line coverages from the AAT section.

The dtda parameter can be used to request a TDA description file. The fmt
parameter is only used for the coordinates.
##sdencl
sdencl: Enclosing boundaries of spatial data.

    sdencl(                                                     
        opt=...,    type of boundary, def. 1                
                    1 = rectangle                           
                    2 = convex hull                         
        fmt=...,    print format for coordinates, def. 10.4 
    ) = output_file;                                            
                                                                            
The command requires a valid spatial data structure defined with the sdnvar
command and calculates an enclosing boundary for all points in all 
(currently selected) type 1, type 2 and type 3 objects. Depending on the
opt parameter this will be a rectangular bounding box or a convex hull.
The coordinates of the boundary are written to the output file as a type 3
object (polygon).              

##sdgen
sdgen: The sdgen command can be used to create a spatial data file based on
a standard data matrix. The syntax is as follows:

    sdgen(                                                         
        id=...,     name of SDID variable (required)               
        xyv=...,    two variables with coordinates (required)      
        av=...,     additional attribute variables (optional)      
        typ=...,    type of spatial objects, def. 1                
        nfmt=...,   integer print format, def. 4                   
        fmt=...,    floating point print format, def. 10.6         
    ) = output_file;                                               

The command requires the definition of an output file on the right-hand side.
Also required is the name of a variable to be used for SDID and two 
variables that contain coordinates. The operation depends on the type of
spatial object specified with the typ parameter.

If typ = 1 (default) the command creates a type 1 object for each record in
the current data matrix. If typ = 2 or typ = 3, the command creates a
corresponding spatial object by using all consecutive records that have
the same value of the variable specified with the id parameter (this is
similar to an operation in block mode). In all cases one can use the av
parameter to add attribute variables to the object description records in
the output file.

##sdgshhs
sdgshhs: This command can be used to convert a GSHHS binary file into a TDA
spatial data file. The syntax is:
                                                                        
    sdgshhs(                                                              
        df=...,     output file (required)                                
        opt=...,    option, def. 1                                        
                    1 = standard coding of longitudes                     
                    2 = longitudes unchanged                              
        level=...,  select polygons with specified level, def. 0          
        fmt=...,    floating point print format, def. 10.5                
        dtda=...,   create TDA description file                           
                                                                        
    ) = gshhs_input_file;                                                 
                                                                        
The input file must be a binary file from the GSHHS data base. For more  
information see Section 9.4.3.2 of the TDA Manual.

##sdinf
sdinf: Information about spatial data.

    sdinf(                                                      
        opt=...,    option, def. 1                              
                    1 = basic information                       
                    2 = additional information about orientation of polygons
        eps=...,    tolerance for area check, def. 1.e-6    
    );
                                                                
This command requires a spatial data matrix defined with the sdnvar command.
By default (opt = 1), the command prints some basic information about the
number of spatial objects, the bounding box and, for type 2 and type 3
objects, about minimal and maximal segment length (euclidean) into the
standard output. If opt = 2, additional information about the orientation
of polygons is written.

##sdnvar
sdnvar: The sdnvar command can be used to read a spatial data file and create
a spatial data matrix. The syntax is as follows:

    sdnvar(
        dfile = ...,    spatial data file           
        noc = ...,      maximal number of object description records, def. 1000
        SDID = ...,     definition of SDID variable
        SDTyp = ...,    definition of SDTyp variable
        SDN = ...,      definition of SDN variable
        SDPtr<5> = rd,  definition of SDPtr variable
        ...
    );

The dfile parameter must be used to specify a spatial data file. Also
required is a defintion of the variables SDID, SDTyp, SDN and SDPtr as the
first four variables. The "dummy" variable SDPtr must have type 5. Following
the standard variables it is possible to define additional variables.

##sdpdata
sdpdata: The command
                                                                         
    sdpdata(                                                  
        opt=...,    option, def. 1                            
                    1 = points                                
                    2 = line segments                         
                    3 = line segments (closed polygons)
        fmt=...,    print format for coordinates, def. 10.4   
    ) = output_file;                                          
                                                                        
can be used to write spatial data to an output file. There are two options.
Option 1 writes one record for each point in all type 1, type 2 and type 3
objects. Each record has three entries:                        
                                                                        
    SDID  x-coordinate  y-coordinate                                      
                                                                        
Option 2 writes one record for each line segment in all type 2 and type 3
objects. Each record has five entries:                         
                                                                        
    SDID  x1-coordinate  y1-coordinate  x1-coordinate  y2-coordinate      
                                                                        
Option 3 writes an additional segment that connects the first and last 
point in a polygon (if not already in the data set). Otherwise it is 
identical with option 2.

See also: pdata.
##sdpgrat
sdpgrat. The sdpgrat command can be used to draw a graticule (meridians and
parallels) based on a projection and plot environment defined with the     
psetupg command.

    sdpgrat(                                                               
        cont=...,   if 1 draw bound of selected region, def. 0             
        gs=...,     greyscale value for cont option, def. 1 (white)        
        lon=...,    sequence of longitudes for meridians                   
        lat=...,    sequence of latitudes for parallels                    
        sc=...,     degrees around pole, def. 10                            
        lt=...,     line typ, def. 1 (solid line)           
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.05 (mm)              
        fsx=...,    font size for labels in x direction, def. 0 (mm)       
        fsy=...,    font size for lables in y direction, def. 0 (mm)       
        fmt=...,    print format for labels, def. 0.0                        
    );                                                                     

##sdplot
sdplot: This command can be used to plot spatial data. It requires a valid
spatial data structure defined with the sdnvar command and a PostScript
2d coordinate system. The command has the following syntax:

    sdplot(                                                    
        opt=...,    option, def. 1                             
                    1 = plot spatial objects                   
                    2 = convex hull for all points             
                    3 = convex hull for each polygon           
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1                          
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 mm                    
        s=...,      number of marker symbol, def. not used     
        fs=...,     size of marker symbol, def. 2 mm           
        gs=...,     greyscale value, def. 1 (white)            
        nc=...,     if nc=1 then no clipping, def. 0           
    );                                                         
                                                                           
Option 1. The operation depends on the type of the spatial objects.              
                                                                           
    Type 1: Points.     This requires the specification of a marker symbol 
                        Otherwise nothing is done.                         
                                                                           
    Type 2: Lines.      If the line width is positive, a line with the     
                        specified line type and width is drawn. Independent
                        of the line width, if a marker symbol is specified,
                        it is plotted for each point in the line.          
                                                                           
    Type 3: Polygons.   Basically the same procedure as for type 2, but    
                        an implicit closepath is added at the end of the     
                        data points. Also, if a valid grey scale value is  
                        specified, the polygon is filled accordingly.      
                                                                           
Option 2. The set of points for all type 1, type 2 and type 3 objects is
taken together and a single convex hull is calculated and plotted. The
drawing uses the line type and line width defined by the lt and lw 
parameters. The gs parameter can be used to fill the convex hull with a
specific greyscale value.

Option 3. A convex hull is plotted for each type 3 object (polygon)
separately. The drawing uses the line type and line width defined by the lt
and lw parameters. The gs parameter can be used to fill the convex hull with
a specific greyscale value.

See also: sdplot31, sdplot32, sdplot33.
##sdplot31
sdplot31. This command plots points in a three-dimensional coordinate system.
The command has the following syntax:

    sdplot31(                                                   
        zval = ..., z value of x-y plane, def. 0.0              
        zvar = ..., optional z coordinates                      
        s=...,      number of marker symbol, def. 0             
        fs=...,     size of marker symbol, def. 2 mm            
        nc=...,     if nc=1 then no clipping, def. 0            
  );                                                          
                                                                          
The command requires a valid spatial data structure defined with the sdnvar
command and a valid PostScript 3d coordinate system defined with the
psetup3 command.
                                                                          
The command considers all type 1 objects (points) in the spatial data   
structure. The output depends on the zvar and s parameters. To explain the
options, assume that a point has coordinates (x,y) and zval specifies a
z coordinate z.                                             
                                                                          
a)  If neither the zvar nor the s parameter is used, nothing is done.   
                                                                          
b)  If the s parameter specifies a valid marker symbol, but the zvar    
    parameter is not used, the symbol is plotted at (x,y,z).            
                                                                          
c)  If the s parameter is not used but the zvar parameter specifies     
    an attribute variable, the value of this variable is taken as a     
    z coordinate for the currently selected point, say z1, and a        
    straight line is drawn from (x,y,z) to (x,y,z1).                    
                                                                          
d)  If both the s and zvar parameters are used, the command first draws 
    a straight line from (x,y,z) to (x,y,z1) and then plots the symbol  
    at position (x,y,z1).                                               
                                                                         
See also: sdplot, sdplot32, sdplot33.
##sdplot32
sdplot32. This command plots lines in a three-dimensional coordinate system.
The command has the following syntax:
                                                                           
    sdplot32(                                                  
        zval = ..., z value of x-y plane, def. 0.0             
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1                          
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 mm                    
        s=...,      number of marker symbol, def. 0            
        fs=...,     size of marker symbol, def. 2 mm           
        nc=...,     if nc=1 then no clipping, def. 0           
    );                                                         
                                                                         
This command requires a valid spatial data structure defined with the sdnvar
command and a valid PostScript 3d coordinate system.            
                                                                         
The command recognizes all type 2 objects (lines) in the spatial data  
structure and, if line width > 0, plots the line in the x-y plane defined
by the z coordinate specified with the zval parameter.         
                                                                           
In addition, if the s parameter specfies a valid marker symbol, this symbol
is plotted at all end points of the segments of the line.         

See also: sdplot, sdplot31, sdplot33.
##sdplot33
sdplot33. This command plots polygons in a three-dimensional coordinate system.
The command has the following syntax:
                                                                           
    sdplot33(                                                  
        zval = ..., z value of x-y plane, def. 0.0             
        zvar = ..., optional z coordinates                     
        lt=...,     line type, def. 1                          
        lw=...,     line width, def. 0.2 mm                    
        s=...,      number of marker symbol, def. 0            
        fs=...,     size of marker symbol, def. 2 mm           
        gs=...,     greyscale value, def. 1 (white)            
        nc=...,     if nc=1 then no clipping, def. 0           
    );                                                         
                                                                         
This command requires a valid spatial data structure defined with the sdnvar
command and a valid PostScript 3d coordinate system.            
                                                                         
The command recognizes all type 3 objects (polygons) in the spatial data
structure. The operation depends on the zvar parameter.   
                                                                         
a) If the zvar parameter is not used, the polygons are plotted in the  
   x-y plane defined by the z-coordinate defined by zval. Given a      
   positive line width, the polygon is plotted with the specified line 
   type and line width. In this case, the gs parameter can be used to  
   specify a grey scale value for filling the area of the polygons.    
                                                                         
   In addition, if the s parameter specfies a valid marker symbol, this
   symbol is plotted at all end points of the segments of the polygon.   
                                                                         
b) If the zvar parameter is used to specify an attribute variable, the 
   values of this variable are taken to define the heights of prisms     
   having the polygons as their bases. The command then tries to plot  
   the prisms in a way that non-visible parts are not shown.           
                                                                         
   Note: The algorithm is based on the possibility to overdraw filled
   regions with the PostScript fill command. In order to work properly,
   it is required that the view parameter in the psetup3 command specifies
   a positive latitude.                                      
                                                                         
   Note also that, in order to work properly, it is required that all    
   prisms have a positive height. Otherwise, they will not be drawn.   
                                                                         
See also: sdplot, sdplot31, sdplot32.
##sdshp
sdshp. Conversion of ESRI shape files into TDA spatial data files.
                                                                        
    sdshp(                                                    
        df=...,     output file                               
        dtda=...,   TDA description file                      
        fmt=...,    floating point print format, def. 12.4      
    ) = path_to_shape_file;                                   
                                                                        
The command reads a shape according to the ESRI Shapefile Technical          
Description, July 1998. The right-hand side must provide a path to the shape
file without a file extension. Given a shape file name abc, it will be 
assumed that there are three files:
                                                                         
    abc.shp   the shape file                                                
    abc.shx   the index file                                                
    abc.dbf   a dBASE file containing descriptions                          
                                                                        
The command reads the files and prints some information into the standard
output. If the df parameter is used to specify an ouptut file, the command    
will write the data to this output file conforming to a standard TDA spatial
data file. The following shape types are recognized:                             
                                                                        
    Type 0 : null shapes without geometrical data (ignored)                
    Type 1 : point                                                        
    Type 3 : polyline                                                     
    Type 5 : polygon                                                      
                                                                        
If the input file contains any other type of shape, the command will exit
with an error message. 

##segr
segr: The segr command can be used to calculate segregation indices.
The syntax is:
                                            
    segr(     
        g=...,        name of variable defining groups   
        fmt=...,      print format, def. 10.4  
        df=...,       output file 
        ptab=...,     additional output file
    ) = varlist;               

##sel
sel: Some of TDA's commands support the parameter sel that can be used
for a local case selection. The syntax is

    sel = expression,

The command will then use only those cases of the data matrix where 
expression evaluated to a nonzero value.
##seq
seq: The command

    seq;

without arguments shows the currently defined sequence data structures.

See also: seqdef, seqdel, seqpd
##seqdef
seqdef: The seqdef command can be used to define a sequence data structure.
The syntax is:

    seqdef(               
        sn=...,     number of sequence data structure, def. 1  
        m=...,      type of input data, def. 1   
        rc=...,     option for recoding states        
        glen=...,   maximal length of internal gaps, def. 0
    ) = varlist;     
                
Possible sequence data structure numbers are sn = 1,...,100.
Possible data types are:

    m = 1 : varlist contains a separate state variable for each time point,
    m = 2 : varlist contains pairs of variables, Y and T, where Y is the
            state and T the starting time in that state.

If m = 1 one can use the glen parameter. If glen > 0, internal gaps 
between identical states and with a length of maximal glen temporal
locations are filled with the surrounding states.

For further information see Section 3.4.2 of the Manual.

See also: seq, seqdel, seqpd
##seqdel
seqdel: The command

    seqdel = n;

deletes sequence data structure number n; the command

    seqdel;

deletes all currently defined sequence data structures.

See also: seq, seqdef, seqpd
##seqev
seqev: The seqev command prints information about events in a set of  
sequence data into an output file. The syntax is:

    seqev(
        sn=...,      number of sequence data structure, def. 1
        sel=...,     expression for sequence selection
    ) = fname;

The command requires at least one valid sequence data structure defined
with the seqdef command.

See also: seqevd
##seqevd
seqevd: The seqevd command has syntax:

    seqevd(
        sn=...,      number of sequence data structure, def. 1
        sel=...,     expression for sequence selection 
        dtda=...,    request TDA description file
    ) = fname;     
             
A file name must be given on the right-hand side, all other parameters are
optional and have their usual meaning. The command creates the specified
output file and writes into this output file a table showing, for each
time point, the number of events occurring at that time point. 

The command requires at least one valid sequence data structure defined
with the seqdef command.

See also: seqev
##seqgc
seqgc: The seqgc command provides basic information about a sequence
data structure, in particular, information about episodes and state  
changes in the sequence. The syntax is:

    seqgc(
        sn=...,     number of sequence data structure, def. 1
        sel=...,    expression for sequence selection 
        v=...,      add variables ... to output file
        dtda=...,   request TDA description file
    ) = fname;

The command requires a valid sequence data structure, see seqdef.

See also: seqlg
##seqlg
seqlg: The seqlg command provides basic information about a sequence
data structure, in particular, information about sequence lengths and
gaps. The syntax is:

    seqlg(             
        sn=...,     number of sequence data structure, def. 1
        sel=...,    expression for sequence selection 
        v=...,      add variables ... to output file
        dtda=...,   request TDA description file
    ) = fname;     

The command requires a valid sequence data structure, see seqdef.

See also: seqgc
##seqm 
seqm: The seqm command can be used to calculate proximity measures for
sequence data based on optimal matching. The syntax is:

    seqm(
        m=...,      selection of method, def. 1
        sn=...,     selection of sequence data structure(s), def. 1
        icost=...,  indel cost specification  
        scost=...,  substitution cost specification  
        rr=1,       use common sequence length 
        sm=...,     option for preprocessing sequences  
        r=...,      random selection of sequences  
        s=...,      print sequence of distances, or LCS  
        cn=...,     compare with specified sequences  
        max=...,    alignment restriction  
        tfmt=...,   print format for distances, def. 5.2   
        v=...,      add variables ... to output file  
        dtda=...,   create TDA description file  
        df=...,     create test output file  
        tst=...,    additional test options 
        fmt=...,    print format for test output file  
    ) = fname;

The command requires a valid sequence data structure, see seqdef.
##seqmd 
seqmd: The seqmd command creates data for regression models based on a
sequence data structure. The syntax is:

    seqmd(
        ev=...,      definition of target event 
        sel=...,     expression for sequence selection 
        tp=...,      definition of time axis 
        v=...,       time-independent covariates          
        xe=...,      event-specific covariates          
        dtda=...,    request TDA description file      
    ) = fname;

For further information see Section 6.18.3 of the Manual.
##seqpd
seqpd: The seqpd command can be used to write sequence data into an
output file. The syntax is:

    seqpd(
        m=...,     structure of output file, def. 1  
        sel=...,   type of input data, def. 1   
        v=...,     add variables ... to output file           
        dtda=...,  request TDA description file          
    ) = fname;      

m=1: the output file will contain a single record for each case in the
     data matrix, and the sequences will be concatenated horizontally.

m=2: the output file contains a separate record for each sequence data 
     structure.

m=3: the output file contains a separate record for each case and time
     point. 

m=4: the output file will be an episode data file based on the first
     currently defined sequence data structure.

m=9: the output fill will contain a single record for each case in the
     data matrix, and then provides, separately for each sequence and state,
     the duration spent in that state.

See also: seq, seqdef, seqdel
##seqpe
seqpe: The seqpe command can be used to derive a sequence data structure
from episode data. The syntax is:

    seqpe(
        id=...,    ID variable    
        org=...,   variable for origin state     
        des=...,   variable for destination state     
        ts=...,    variable for starting time    
        tf=...,    variable for ending time      
        tp=...,    definition of time points    
        m=...,     missing value, def. -1
        m1=...,    missing value at the beginning, def. -1
        m2=...,    missing value at the end, def. -1
        v=...,     add variables ... to output file 
    ) = fname;     

All parameters, except m, m1, m2, and v, are required. The resulting sequence
data are written into the output file fname.

See also: seqdef
##seqpm 
seqpm: The seqpm command can be used for pattern matching in sequences.  
The syntax is:

    seqpm(                                                          
        sn = ...,           number of sequence data structure, def. 1   
        ps = [...],[...],   definition of max 20 patterns               
        df = ...,           test output file                            
        nfmt=...,           integer print format, def. 4  
        v=...,              add ... variables to output file            
        dtda=...,           TDA description file                        
    ) = fname;              name of output file                         
                                                                        
    Patterns must be given as follows:                            
                                                                      
        ps = [a1,a2,...],[b1,b2,...],...                              
                                                                      
    where a1,a2,..., b1,b2,... are one of the folowing characters:

        nonnegative integers for valid states                     
        ?    matches any character                                
        *    matches any sequence of characters                   
        +    any repeat of the previous character                 
        -    any sequence of identical states

    The contents of the output file can be seen from the TDA
    description file.
##seqrd
seqrd: The seqrd command can be used to create simple random sequences.
The syntax is:

    seqrd(            
        ns=...,     number of states, def. 2  
        len=...,    length of sequences, def. 1  
        noc=...,    number of sequences, def. 1000        
    ) = fname;     

The resulting sequences are written into the output file fname.
##seqsd
seqsd: The seqsd command calculates state distributions based on a  
sequence data structure. The syntax is:

    seqsd(
        sn=...,      number of sequence data structure, def. 1
        sel=...,     expression for sequence selection 
        dtda=...,    request TDA description file
    ) = fname;

The command requires a valid sequence data structure, see seqdef.
##seqsi
seqsi: The seqsi command can be used to create a state indicator matrix.
The syntax is:

    seqsi(             
        tp=...,     definition of time points         
        m=...,      option for case selection, def. 1  
        v=...,      add variables ... to output file           
        dtda=...,   request TDA description file        
    ) = fname;     

The command uses the first of the currently defined sequence data
structures. For further information see Section 3.4.5 of the Manual.
##sequence data
sequence data: Up to 100 sequence data structures can be simultaneously
defined with the seqdef command.

See also: seq, seqdel, seqpd, seqpe, seqrd, seqsi.
##shell
shell: See: $

##silent
silent: The silent commands modifies the writing of output text.

    silent = 0;     writes all output (default)
             1;     suppresses standard output
             2;     suppresses standard error output
             3;     suppresses both standard, and standard error output
            -1;     enables output from matrix and loop commands

The command has no influence on "fatal" error messages and writing
external files. It cannot be used in interactive mode.
##sma
sma: The sma command can be used to calculate moving averages for a
set of variables. The syntax is:

    sma(      
        gss=...,   specification of weights       
        opt=...,   treatment of end values, def. 1   
                    1 = copy-on end-values   
                    2 = replicate end values   
        r=...,     repeat factor, def. 1   
        fmt=...,   print format, def. 10.4   
        df=...,    optional output file         
        sel=...,   optional case selection            
    ) = varlist;    

##smd
smd: The command

    smd(     
        sm=[...],   sequence of operations    
        opt=...,    treatment of end values, def. 1    
                     1 = copy-on end-values    
                     2 = replicate end values   
                     3 = E rule    
        fmt=...,    print format, def. 10.4    
        df=...,     optional output file         
        sel=...,    optional case selection         
    ) = varlist;     

calculates running median smoothers for the variables given in varlist.
The syntax for the sm parameters is

    sm=[c1,c2c3,...], 

where each ci is one of the following characters:

    2  2-median
    3  3-median
    4  4-median
    5  5-median
    H  hanning
    R  replicate until no more changes
    S  splitting
    t  combine smooth and rough     

For more information see Section 5.5.2 of the Manual.
##smoothing
smoothing: See the following commands:

    sma     moving averages
    smd     running median smoothers
    spl     smoothing splines

##sort
sort: See: pdata, esort

##spatial data
spatial data: This expression refers to a data matrix created with the sdnvar
command. The following commands are available for spatial data:

    sddcwp      conversion of DCW polygon point data files
    sde00       conversion of Arc/Info export files
    sdencl      enclosing boundaries of spatial data 
    sdgen       creates a spatial data file
    sdgshhs     conversion of GSHHS data files
    sdinf       information about spatial data
    sdnvar      creates a spatial data matrix
    sdpdata     writes spatial data to output file
    sdplot      plots spatial objects in a 2d coordinate system 
    sdplot31    plots type 1 objects in a 3d coordinate system
    sdplot32    plots type 2 objects in a 3d coordinate system
    sdplot33    plots type 3 objects in a 3d coordinate system
    sdshp       conversion of ESRI shape files

Also, the pdata command provides an option to write spatial data to an
output file. Furthermore, there are some commands to set up a geographical
coordinate system for subsequent plots:

    psetupg     specifies a projection and plot environment     
    sdpgrat     draws meridians and parallels                   
    sdpgeo      draws points and lines                          
    sdpmap      draws spatial objects (points, lines, polygons) 

The following type 1 operators can be used to create variables based on
the coordinates of spatial data objects.

    sdxmin      minimum of x coordinates
    sdxmax      maximum of x coordinates
    sdymin      minimum of y coordinates
    sdymax      maximum of y coordinates
    sdarea      area of polygon (0 for type 1 and 2 objects)
    sdp(x,y)    1 if (x,y) is contained in the spatial object

Note that these operators cannot be used with the sdnvar command but require
an additional nvar command.

##spl
spl: The spl command can be used to calculated a smoothing spline for
a pair of variables, X and Y, optionally weighted with a variable W.
The syntax is:

    spl(     
        sig=...,     smoothing factor, def. 0.0    
        deg=...,     degree of spline, def. 2    
        max=...,     max number of knots, def. see text    
        mxit=...,    max number of iterations, def. 10   
        fmt=...,     print format, def. 10.4   
        df=...,      output file         
        rx=a(d)b,    range for interpolation          
    ) = X,Y [,W];     

For more information see Section 5.5.3 of the Manual.
##spss
spss: See the commands rspss and wspss for reading and writing SPSS
portable files. 

See also: pdata
##stata
stata: See the commands rstata and wstata for reading and writing     
Stata files. 
##step function
step function: see plot and plotp
##string variable
string variable: String variables may be part of TDA's data matrix.
They are identified by variable type 1. String variables always have
a fixed length. Note that only a limited number of commands and
operators can make sense of string variables. Whenever TDA expects
a numerical variable and finds a string variable, the numerical
value of the string is taken to be zero. Currently, string variables
are only recognized by the pdata command which then prints the strings
to the output file.

a) In order to create string variables with the nvar command one can use
the operator

    Variable_name = str(n,m),

This requires that one has specified a data file with the dfile parameter.
TDA then creates a string variable with the specified name and uses the
columns n,...,m in the data file to create the strings.

Note that the str operator cannot be combined with any other operators. 

b) The rspss and rstata commands now recognize string variables which may
be present in an SPSS portable file, or Stata file, and creates corresponding
TDA string variables as part of TDA's data matrix.

c) TDA data archives may contain string variables. The type of the variable
must be specified in the variable description file with the format entry. In
general, each line in a variable description file has the following syntax:

    VNAME is the name of the variable.
    FN    is the logical file number.
    OFF   is the offset in the data file records where the
          variable begins (counting begins with column 0).
    FMT   is a format information which must be given in one
          of the following three ways:

          n.m   where n is a positive integer and m is a non-zero
                integer. n is used as the length (number of
                physical columns) for the variable. n.m is used
                as the print format.

          n     where n is a positive integer. Again, n is used
                as the length (number of physical columns) for the
                variable, and the print format is n.0

          n     where n is a negative integer. The variable is
                then recognized as a string variable with length -n.

d) The pdata command recognizes string variables. They are written as strings
into the output file. 

e) There are a few operators that can be used to create numerical variables
from string variables. See: strlen, strv, strvp, strsp.

##strlen
strlen. The type 1 operator

    strlen(S)

returns the storage size (length) of the string variable S.            

##strv
strv: The type 1 operator

    strv(S)

assumes that S is a string that only consists of digits. If this is the
case the operator returns the corresponding numerical value; otherwise
the operator returns -1. The storage size of the resulting variable
depends on the length of S.                             

##strvp
strvp: The type 1 operator

    strvp(S,n,m)

creates a numerical variable from columns n,...,m of the string variable S.
If it is not possible to convert the sub-string to a numerical value the
operator returns -1.

##strsp
strsp: The type 2 operator 

    strsp(S)

creates a numerical variable that sorts the strings in S in ascending
order.

##system file
system file: See rsys for reading, and wsys for writing, TDA system files.
##time
time: The command

    time;

without arguments writes the current date and time into the standard
output.
##time periods
time periods: Commands that require a definition of time periods
generally use the tp, or t, parameter with syntax

    tp = t1,t2,t3,...,

where t1,t2,... is an ascending sequence of time points, and t1 >= 0.
Alternatively, one can use the syntax

    tp = t1 (d) t2,

in order to specify a sequence t1, t1 + d, t1 + 2d, ..., until t2.
##tree
tree: Trees are a special case of graph data, see Section 3.6.6 of the
Manual. Specific commands for trees are:

    ptree, pltree

##tsel            
tsel: The command

    tsel = expression;

selects all cases (rows) in the current data matrix where expression has
a nonzero value. Alternatively, one can use the syntax      
                
    tsel = matrix_expression;

matrix_expression must result in a NOCDM x 1 matrix where NOCDM is the       
number of cases in the data matrix (before any case selection). 

For example, creating a NOCDM x 1 matrix S, the command

    tsel = S;

would select all data matrix rows i where S(i) is not equal to zero.
The current value of NOC would then be equal to the number of non-zero 
entries in S.

Case selection with the tsel command remains active until:
    
-   A new tsel command substitutes the previous one.
-   The current case selection is explicitly turned off with: tsel=off;    
-   It follows a command that automatically turns off any active
    temporary case selection (nvar, ndvar, recode).

The tsel command implies deallocation of any data structures which depend
on case selection. In particular, data structures for episode, sequence
and relational data will be deallocated if there is a request for a new
temporary case selection. The tsel command also implies a re-evaluation
of case weights, if defined, see cwt.                    
##unf
unf: The unf command can be used for unfolding of rank data. The syntax is

    unf(                                                          
        df=...,         create output file                        
    ) = X1,...,Xm;      variables.                                 

    Values of the variables are interpreted as follows. If the values
    for case i are: xi1,...,xim, this means:                    
    a)  alternative j is preferred to alternative k if xij > xik 
    b)  indifference if xij = xik.                                

    The command checks all m! permutations of the m alternatives
    (actually only half of them) in order to find the best permutation
    for an alignment of the input data based on Kendall's tau.
    Finally, the best permutation is reported in the standard
    output. The df option can be used to request an output file
    that contains one record for each case: (1) case number,
    (2) best aligned rank order, (3) input data, (4) a flag that
    is 1 if (2) and (3) are different.

##variable
variable: Variable names consist of up to 32 characters which may consist
of the following letters:                               
                                                        
    A,...,Z, a,...,z, 0,1,...,9, _, @, $

The first character must be an upper case letter or one of the            
characters: _, @, $. The other characters may be upper or lower case.
Note that variable names are case sensitive.

Each variable has an associated type. There are the following types:

    1   string variable,
    2   numerical constant,
    3   standard numerical variables that do not directly or
        indirectly depend on type 2 operators,
    4   numerical variables that directly or indirectly depend on
        type 2 operators,
    5   variables created by the edef command.

See also: nvar
##while
while: The while/endwhile pair of commands can be used for conditional
repetition of commands. The syntax is:

    while (expression);
    ...
    ...
    endwhile;

The commands in between while and endwhile are executed while expression
is true. Expression can be any valid scalar expression (including
standard expressions). TDA interprets the expression as true if the absolute
value of expression is greater than

    sqrt(EPSI)  [approx 1.e-8]

where EPSI is the machine precision.  

while/endwhile pairs can be nested and also mixed with repeat/endrepeat
pairs. The maximal level of nesting is 100.

See also: break, repeat, if      
##wspss
wspss: The wspss command can be used to create an SPSS portable file.
The syntax is:

    wspss(      
        keep=...,    keep variables     
        drop=...,    drop variables             
        sort=...,    sort cases
    ) = fname;             

See also: wspss1, rspss, rspss1

##wspss1
wspss1: The wspss1 command can be used to create an SPSS sav file.
The syntax is:

    wspss1(                                                 
        keep = varlist,                                             
        drop = varlist,                                             
        sort = varlist,                                             
    ) = output_file_name;                                           

This command writes TDA's internal data matrix (or the variables selected with
the keep or drop option) into an output file that can be used as an SPSS sav
file. Note that this is a binary file; storage of numerical data conforms to
an INTEL platform. Note also that string variables cannot be used for sorting.

See also: wspss, rspss, rspss1.

##wstata
wstata: The wstata command can be used to create a Stata file.
The syntax is:

    wstata(     
        keep=...,    keep variables
        drop=...,    drop variables
        sort=...,    sort cases
        ptyp=...,    kind of output file, def. 10
                     4 : for Stata release 4
                     6 : for Stata release 6
                     7 : for Stata release 7
                    10 : for Stata release 10   
        n=...,       treatment of variable names
                     1 : default
                     2 : translate to lower case letters
                     3 : translate to upper case letters
    ) = fname;                  

See also: rstata

##wsys
wsys: The command

    wsys [= name of a TDA system file];

creates a TDA system file. The default file name is "tda.sys".

See also: rsys
##xconh  
xconh: The command

    xconh(
        lt = ...,       line type, def. 1
        lw = ...,       line width, def. 0.2
    );

adds convex hulls to the most recent scatterplot. If the scatterplot
consists of more than one group, the convex hulls are plotted
separately for each group.

See also: xplot, xplotf, xf
##xdelete
xdelete: The command

    xdelete = n1,n2,...;

deletes the plot objects n1,n2,... from the currently active PostScript file.
##xf     
xf: The command

    xf(     
        rx = a(d)b,     definition of x axis     
        lt=...,         line type, def. 1
        lw=...,         line width, def. 0.2 mm
        pxlen=...,      length of x axis, def. 120 mm 
        pylen=...,      length of y axis, def.  80 mm

    ) = function(x);

plots function(x). If there is a currently active PostScript file the
function is plotted for the corresponding x axis. Otherwise, the command
creates a new PostSript file (named xplot.ps). By default, the function 
is then plotted for an x axis from 1 to 10. Alternatively, one can define
the x axis with

    rx = a (d) b,

The range is then from a to b, with increments d.
##xlog
xlog: The command

    xlog [= psfile];

shows the plot objects in the currently open PostScript file or, 
optionally, in the PostScript file specified on the right-hand side.
##xopen
xopen: The command

    xopen = name_of_PostScript_file;

tries to open a new PostScript file. This must be a PostScript file
created with TDA.
##xplot  
xplot: The command

    xplot(
        opt=...,        1 (default) scatterplot, 2 line plot
        s=...,          symbol type, def. 1
        fs=...,         symbol size, def. 1.3 mm
        lt=...,         line type, def. 1
        lw=...,         line width, def. 0.2 mm
        pxlen=...,      length of x axis, def. 120 mm 
        pylen=...,      length of y axis, def.  80 mm
    ) = X,Y [,G];

creates a scatterplot based on the variables X and Y. A third variable,
G, is optional. If added, each set of data points having the same value
of G is treated as a separate group and is shown by a separate plot symbol.
All other parameters are optional. Note: the s parameter can only be used
when there is just one group.      

See also: xplotf, xreg
##xplotf 
xplotf: The command

    xplotf(
        cn=...,         column numbers of variables, def. cn=1,2
        gn=...,         column number of group variable 
        opt=...,        1 (default) scatterplot, 2 line plot
        s=...,          symbol type, def. 1
        fs=...,         symbol size, def. 1.3 mm
        lt=...,         line type, def. 1
        lw=...,         line width, def. 0.2 mm
        pxlen=...,      length of x axis, def. 120 mm 
        pylen=...,      length of y axis, def.  80 mm

    ) = name_of_data_file;

This command works similar to the xplot command but gets its data 
diretly from a data file assumed to be a standard free format data file
with valid end of line characters.

1)  By default, the xplotf command uses the first two data columns
    in the input file to get values for the X and Y variables.

2)  Optionally, one can use the cn parameter to specify column numbers:

        cn = c1,c2,c3,...,

    defining corresponding variables X1,X2,X3,... This specifies the
    data point groups

        (X1,X2), (X1,X3), ...

    and a scatterplot, or line plot, is created separately for each group.

3)  As a further option, one can specify

        cn = c1,c2, gn=c3,

    defining corresponding variables X, Y, and G, respectively. X and Y
    define the data points, and G is a grouping variable, meaning that
    each set of (X,Y) values which have the same value in G is treated
    as a separate group.

See also: xplot, xreg
##xreg   
xreg: The command

    xreg(
       lt = ...,       line type, def. 1
       lw = ...,       line width, def. 0.2
       sig = ...,      sigma for lowess, def. 0.5
    ) [= n];

adds a regression line to the most recent scatterplot. In case of more
than one group, this is done separately for each group.

    n = 1       Lowess, default
    n = 2       LS regression
    n = 3       L1 norm regression

See also: xf
##xshow
xshow. The command

    xshow(dscal=...) [= psfile];

allows a preview of the currently active PostScript file, or psfile if
given as an argument. The optional dscal parameter can be used to change
the default scaling factor, dscal=1.

This command works fine in an X windows environment. It opens a plot window
and shows the plot until the window is closed by typing any key. A somewhat
limited version of the command is available for Windows NT.

Note that the xshow command interprets the currently defined PostScript file
but has only limited capabilities for interpreting PostScript commands. In
fact, the interpreter is heavily tied to a special format used for TDA
PostScript files. If one needs to preview the complete PostScript file one
should not use xshow but, e.g., GhostScript or GhostView.
##zoo
zoo: Zoo, written by Rahul Dhesi, is a general purpose program to maintain
compressed data archives. The syntax is
 
    zoo commands [archive_name] [file1 file2 ...]  

Some useful commands are:

zoo h              displays a short list of commands to be used with Zoo  

zoo l exam.zoo     lists the contents of the archive exam.zoo 

zoo ah exam fname  adds the file fname to the archive exam.zoo

zoo x exam fname   extracts the file fname from exam.zoo (if no file name
                   is given (zoo x exam) all files contained in  the        
                   archive are extracted.

zoo D exam fname   deletes the file fname from the archive.

zoo P exam         rebuilds the archive exam.zoo with all background files
                   actually deleted.    

#------------###################

